01:19:11.095 --> 01:19:13.595
So let me get started with a broader question.

01:19:14.325 --> 01:19:16.665
Will we continue to benefit from scaling

01:19:16.725 --> 01:19:17.905
foundation models?

01:19:19.105 --> 01:19:21.365
I believe the answer to this is debatable.

01:19:22.135 --> 01:19:24.294
But it is useful to think about

01:19:24.294 --> 01:19:26.285
if the answer turns out to be no,

01:19:26.604 --> 01:19:27.964
then what is the reason?

01:19:29.394 --> 01:19:31.624
At a high level, scaling depends

01:19:31.624 --> 01:19:33.695
on two factors. Data

01:19:33.695 --> 01:19:34.515
and compute.

01:19:35.874 --> 01:19:38.064
So We have a brief good

01:19:38.064 --> 01:19:40.115
sense that compute is getting too

01:19:40.355 --> 01:19:41.975
cheaper and is getting more accessible

01:19:42.434 --> 01:19:44.855
over time, so maybe compute is not a problem.

01:19:45.684 --> 01:19:47.765
But data is a different story.

01:19:47.765 --> 01:19:50.005
We hear that data is running

01:19:50.005 --> 01:19:52.195
out. Internet data is finite.

01:19:52.705 --> 01:19:54.865
And the speed that we generate

01:19:54.865 --> 01:19:56.245
data may not be

01:19:56.955 --> 01:19:59.295
faster than the speed we want model capabilities

01:19:59.595 --> 01:20:01.815
to grow. So data is a real bottleneck.

01:20:02.915 --> 01:20:05.155
When it comes to data running out, we think

01:20:05.155 --> 01:20:07.175
about the quantity of the data and

01:20:07.565 --> 01:20:09.615
we have been doing great research on

01:20:10.015 --> 01:20:12.275
topics like synthetic data generation

01:20:12.335 --> 01:20:14.385
to increase the data. But

01:20:14.385 --> 01:20:16.625
in this talk, I wanna offer a different

01:20:16.625 --> 01:20:17.125
perspective.

01:20:19.275 --> 01:20:20.995
Maybe in terms of quantity,

01:20:21.375 --> 01:20:23.695
we are we are fine. We in fact

01:20:23.695 --> 01:20:25.724
may have a lot of data. But

01:20:25.725 --> 01:20:27.885
the real problem is the data in

01:20:27.885 --> 01:20:29.505
the world is fragmented.

01:20:30.405 --> 01:20:32.485
Meaning these are the data sets owned by

01:20:32.485 --> 01:20:34.745
different institutions or different entities.

01:20:35.055 --> 01:20:37.135
And there is no single central

01:20:37.135 --> 01:20:39.075
entity that have centralized access

01:20:39.445 --> 01:20:41.440
to all the datasets. But

01:20:41.685 --> 01:20:43.765
So what does it exactly mean? So we

01:20:43.765 --> 01:20:45.755
all know that valuable datasets

01:20:46.475 --> 01:20:48.555
are critical for foundation model training. They

01:20:48.555 --> 01:20:50.614
should be high quality, they should be

01:20:50.615 --> 01:20:52.755
broad coverage, very

01:20:52.755 --> 01:20:53.735
diverse in domains.

01:20:55.175 --> 01:20:57.195
But also, valuable data such are often

01:20:57.255 --> 01:20:59.375
not public. Valuable

01:20:59.435 --> 01:21:01.215
data sets are often restricted,

01:21:02.705 --> 01:21:03.765
private, or proprietary

01:21:05.435 --> 01:21:07.595
that some institutions or entities may own

01:21:07.835 --> 01:21:10.155
and may not want to share for public

01:21:10.155 --> 01:21:12.275
use. There could

01:21:12.275 --> 01:21:14.435
be many different reasons for that, they may

01:21:14.435 --> 01:21:16.915
have want to retain having full control

01:21:16.915 --> 01:21:19.075
over the data or having full

01:21:19.075 --> 01:21:21.134
value of the data. And

01:21:21.135 --> 01:21:22.995
some examples include corporate

01:21:23.455 --> 01:21:25.825
code, some textbooks owned by

01:21:26.125 --> 01:21:28.055
publishers, internal documentations,

01:21:28.515 --> 01:21:30.614
or medical record. And you

01:21:30.615 --> 01:21:32.854
can see there are often on

01:21:32.854 --> 01:21:34.785
highly specialized domains, which are

01:21:35.025 --> 01:21:37.425
actually very important because nowadays foundation

01:21:37.425 --> 01:21:39.755
models are increasingly targeting those

01:21:39.755 --> 01:21:41.915
specialized domain. So

01:21:42.715 --> 01:21:44.835
On top of that, data

01:21:44.835 --> 01:21:47.054
that used to be public is now

01:21:47.055 --> 01:21:48.835
increasingly getting closed.

01:21:49.975 --> 01:21:52.235
This is a graph from a paper called Consonant Crisis

01:21:52.295 --> 01:21:54.475
that shows that data

01:21:54.475 --> 01:21:56.715
that used to be included in common crawl which

01:21:56.715 --> 01:21:58.775
is public, is now getting closed

01:21:58.775 --> 01:22:00.855
because data owners do not want to

01:22:00.855 --> 01:22:03.355
use their data for public use.

01:22:03.715 --> 01:22:05.794
So this means even data that used to

01:22:05.795 --> 01:22:07.705
be public is closing the news

01:22:08.104 --> 01:22:10.424
public data is not just not growing,

01:22:10.425 --> 01:22:12.535
but also shrinking. So this is

01:22:12.535 --> 01:22:14.635
a big problem. So

01:22:14.635 --> 01:22:16.715
what do I do in terms of model training?

01:22:16.715 --> 01:22:18.825
So let's start with reviewing

01:22:18.825 --> 01:22:20.605
how current LLM training works.

01:22:21.635 --> 01:22:23.815
So we gather data from many different

01:22:23.955 --> 01:22:26.365
sources, but we typically treat it as one

01:22:26.845 --> 01:22:28.924
set of data. We just gather all

01:22:28.925 --> 01:22:31.215
of them into a single set, We

01:22:31.434 --> 01:22:33.575
randomly shuffle them, and we use it

01:22:33.575 --> 01:22:35.755
to train a single giant model.

01:22:36.615 --> 01:22:38.854
So this is great, but this assumes

01:22:38.854 --> 01:22:40.885
there is a single entity,

01:22:40.885 --> 01:22:42.985
which is a model developer that has

01:22:43.505 --> 01:22:45.545
centralized access to all the data.

01:22:46.105 --> 01:22:48.265
However, in reality, that's not really

01:22:48.265 --> 01:22:50.045
true because datasets are

01:22:50.365 --> 01:22:52.604
fragmented. They're distributed. They are owned

01:22:52.605 --> 01:22:54.804
by different entities. And current

01:22:54.805 --> 01:22:56.765
training doesn't really support scenario.

01:22:57.294 --> 01:22:59.455
So that's kind of the main claim in this talk,

01:22:59.455 --> 01:23:01.495
which is we really need a

01:23:01.495 --> 01:23:03.515
new training recipe for LLM

01:23:03.655 --> 01:23:05.795
that can benefit from fragmented

01:23:05.795 --> 01:23:07.894
data. Okay.

01:23:08.355 --> 01:23:10.595
So now how do we do that? So

01:23:10.595 --> 01:23:13.015
we had a proposal that is about modular

01:23:13.155 --> 01:23:15.184
distributed training and here's how

01:23:15.184 --> 01:23:17.344
it works. So we first have a

01:23:17.345 --> 01:23:19.445
public model that is

01:23:19.445 --> 01:23:21.825
trained on public data only like Common Core.

01:23:22.624 --> 01:23:24.965
Then we also there is a set of data owners

01:23:25.184 --> 01:23:27.415
who have their own data locally.

01:23:27.854 --> 01:23:30.115
They can take this public model and then

01:23:30.254 --> 01:23:32.543
do continuous training on their data

01:23:32.544 --> 01:23:34.315
locally. So this training is

01:23:34.555 --> 01:23:36.655
completely in isolation, completely independent.

01:23:37.305 --> 01:23:39.385
And they never share the data to public,

01:23:39.385 --> 01:23:40.765
the data is always local.

01:23:42.105 --> 01:23:43.735
Once this training is done, then

01:23:44.395 --> 01:23:46.475
they still don't share data, but they can share

01:23:46.475 --> 01:23:48.645
model parameters. And we can combine

01:23:48.705 --> 01:23:50.685
it into a single unified model.

01:23:52.045 --> 01:23:54.545
So this proposal have two unique properties.

01:23:55.015 --> 01:23:57.175
First, it allows data owners to be

01:23:57.175 --> 01:23:58.705
able to contribute to AI

01:23:59.265 --> 01:24:01.425
training without needing to share the raw data. So

01:24:01.425 --> 01:24:03.745
they can still retain full control

01:24:03.805 --> 01:24:06.045
over the data. They can retain full

01:24:06.045 --> 01:24:07.065
value of the data.

01:24:08.105 --> 01:24:10.135
Now other thing is as you can see, this

01:24:10.135 --> 01:24:12.505
training is completely modular. That means

01:24:12.665 --> 01:24:14.825
adding data or removing the data is

01:24:14.825 --> 01:24:17.135
very easy. For instance, if one

01:24:17.135 --> 01:24:18.434
data owner want to

01:24:20.725 --> 01:24:22.764
and put it to the

01:24:22.765 --> 01:24:24.995
combined model. If one of the

01:24:24.995 --> 01:24:27.315
data owners that used to participate wanna opt

01:24:27.795 --> 01:24:29.875
out later on, we can do that simply by

01:24:29.875 --> 01:24:32.015
removing parameters contributed by

01:24:32.015 --> 01:24:34.275
data owner. And the rest of the model is

01:24:34.415 --> 01:24:36.635
guaranteed not to use any of that data.

01:24:37.995 --> 01:24:40.115
So this is nice. But how

01:24:40.115 --> 01:24:42.515
do we actually achieve this? What is our actual

01:24:42.515 --> 01:24:44.975
technical solution? So let's consider

01:24:44.975 --> 01:24:47.295
all the options. Let's start with, modern

01:24:47.295 --> 01:24:49.425
emerging techniques. So

01:24:49.425 --> 01:24:51.905
model merging is broadly useful for merging

01:24:51.905 --> 01:24:54.015
a few different models in our case,

01:24:54.255 --> 01:24:56.575
we have one public model, and then

01:24:56.575 --> 01:24:58.385
let's say three models trained

01:24:59.664 --> 01:25:01.695
four of them. And then we can combine

01:25:01.695 --> 01:25:03.774
them into a single model using weight

01:25:03.775 --> 01:25:05.385
averaging called model soup.

01:25:06.025 --> 01:25:08.055
Or doing ensemble. These

01:25:08.055 --> 01:25:09.915
model merging techniques are actually

01:25:10.215 --> 01:25:12.254
already being used as a standard practice

01:25:12.254 --> 01:25:14.684
for boosting model performance. But

01:25:14.685 --> 01:25:16.764
typically, these models are trained on

01:25:16.765 --> 01:25:18.845
near identical data sets, but

01:25:18.845 --> 01:25:20.225
with slightly different distribution.

01:25:21.525 --> 01:25:23.765
We found that actually in our case,

01:25:23.765 --> 01:25:26.025
things are a little different because datasets

01:25:26.255 --> 01:25:28.595
from different data owners are completely disjoint

01:25:29.055 --> 01:25:31.065
datasets. And can have very different

01:25:31.065 --> 01:25:33.385
distribution. And we found model merging

01:25:33.385 --> 01:25:35.445
doesn't work in this case

01:25:35.445 --> 01:25:37.625
because when models are trained on completely

01:25:37.685 --> 01:25:40.105
disjoint datasets, their model

01:25:40.485 --> 01:25:42.585
weight can diverge so much that

01:25:42.585 --> 01:25:44.174
it becomes very difficult to merge.

01:25:45.695 --> 01:25:48.035
So that what do we do? So we consider

01:25:48.335 --> 01:25:50.175
second option, which is MOE merging.

01:25:50.895 --> 01:25:52.975
And to talk about MOE merging, we should

01:25:52.975 --> 01:25:53.715
first talk about

01:25:56.205 --> 01:25:58.164
k. So this is a model that is

01:25:58.645 --> 01:26:00.805
not on MOE. We call it a dense model.

01:26:00.885 --> 01:26:03.044
So it has a few small layers like multi

01:26:03.045 --> 01:26:04.425
annotation and then on FFmpeg.

01:26:05.175 --> 01:26:07.515
And usually, consists of the majority of parameters.

01:26:08.965 --> 01:26:11.204
Now what Mixr of Experts does is

01:26:11.205 --> 01:26:12.665
to replace this FFN

01:26:13.695 --> 01:26:15.875
into a set of small FFNs

01:26:17.195 --> 01:26:19.615
many small FFNs called expert,

01:26:20.325 --> 01:26:22.455
and then also a router. So how it

01:26:22.455 --> 01:26:24.864
works is only a subset of those

01:26:26.785 --> 01:26:28.765
router. And

01:26:29.084 --> 01:26:31.505
DTM was our very standard architecture

01:26:31.725 --> 01:26:33.425
in all frontier models now,

01:26:34.165 --> 01:26:35.625
They use it because

01:26:36.185 --> 01:26:38.245
of compute efficiency. You can see

01:26:38.245 --> 01:26:40.225
here that MOE have nice modular

01:26:40.445 --> 01:26:42.505
architecture So our goal is can we

01:26:42.505 --> 01:26:44.845
actually benefit from this modular architecture?

01:26:46.385 --> 01:26:48.465
So what we wanna do is something like this.

01:26:48.465 --> 01:26:50.475
So again, we have to four models,

01:26:50.475 --> 01:26:52.735
and we want to merge it into a single

01:26:52.955 --> 01:26:55.305
MOE. We do that by keeping

01:26:55.365 --> 01:26:57.524
every FFN separate, so

01:26:57.525 --> 01:26:59.155
we can keep the x coming

01:26:59.615 --> 01:27:01.834
from each data owner as it is. And

01:27:01.835 --> 01:27:04.144
then do weight weight averaging for

01:27:04.145 --> 01:27:06.225
the shared layers like multi attention

01:27:06.225 --> 01:27:08.275
and so on. And then we had a router

01:27:08.275 --> 01:27:10.434
later on. So first of all,

01:27:10.434 --> 01:27:12.934
this architecture is actually exactly identical

01:27:13.155 --> 01:27:15.245
to the standard m way, which is

01:27:15.245 --> 01:27:17.345
a great thing because we can reuse

01:27:17.485 --> 01:27:19.705
the existing infrastructure for

01:27:19.925 --> 01:27:22.064
MOE serving or inference. That's a great

01:27:22.065 --> 01:27:24.295
thing. However, there is a problem,

01:27:24.355 --> 01:27:26.525
which is, as you can see, model

01:27:26.525 --> 01:27:28.605
training is done as a dense model. So

01:27:28.605 --> 01:27:30.245
this model actually never

01:27:30.644 --> 01:27:32.724
learned how to be an MOE model. So

01:27:32.725 --> 01:27:34.584
typically, you need to further

01:27:35.015 --> 01:27:37.175
train on MOE after merging, showing

01:27:37.175 --> 01:27:39.655
to the overall data set. However,

01:27:39.715 --> 01:27:41.924
that's not really applicable in our setup.

01:27:41.925 --> 01:27:43.705
Because we assume datasets

01:27:44.165 --> 01:27:46.025
are completely disjoint and fragmented.

01:27:46.705 --> 01:27:48.785
No single entity can have access to

01:27:48.785 --> 01:27:50.855
all the datasets. So now

01:27:50.855 --> 01:27:52.085
the question is,

01:27:53.535 --> 01:27:55.735
MOE but remove the need for joint

01:27:55.735 --> 01:27:56.634
data apps?

01:27:57.835 --> 01:27:58.735
Is that clear?

01:28:00.315 --> 01:28:02.555
Cool. Okay. So we came over a couple

01:28:02.555 --> 01:28:04.675
of techniques to deal with this

01:28:04.675 --> 01:28:06.755
issue. One of them is what we call

01:28:06.755 --> 01:28:08.765
learning to coordinate. And this

01:28:08.765 --> 01:28:11.004
is how it works. So when a data owner

01:28:11.005 --> 01:28:12.905
does their own training, they

01:28:13.365 --> 01:28:15.785
take the dense model, And instead

01:28:16.005 --> 01:28:18.254
of doing regular continual training, which would

01:28:18.255 --> 01:28:20.485
override the model weight, we are going

01:28:20.485 --> 01:28:22.565
to actually make it on MOE with two

01:28:22.565 --> 01:28:24.674
experts. And in particular, we

01:28:24.675 --> 01:28:26.274
are going to freeze

01:28:26.835 --> 01:28:29.075
the first FFN as well as all the shared

01:28:29.075 --> 01:28:31.115
layers and only keep the second

01:28:31.655 --> 01:28:33.885
FFN trainable. Gonna talk about

01:28:33.885 --> 01:28:36.045
the router later on, so let's forget about the

01:28:36.045 --> 01:28:38.075
router for now. So you

01:28:38.075 --> 01:28:40.255
can see that this is actually identical

01:28:40.555 --> 01:28:42.764
to having the the

01:28:43.005 --> 01:28:45.084
the public model as it is as an

01:28:45.084 --> 01:28:46.705
anchor model. It's frozen.

01:28:47.115 --> 01:28:49.595
But then just add an additional FFN

01:28:49.595 --> 01:28:51.135
to be trained on this

01:28:51.785 --> 01:28:53.865
their local data sets. So it's

01:28:53.865 --> 01:28:55.515
kind of learning how to coordinate

01:28:55.995 --> 01:28:57.865
and learning how to work as an MOE

01:28:58.425 --> 01:29:00.655
by using this public model as an anchor.

01:29:01.265 --> 01:29:03.425
Once this training is completed, this data owner

01:29:03.425 --> 01:29:05.434
can just contribute the second

01:29:05.434 --> 01:29:07.655
back to the public space and we

01:29:07.655 --> 01:29:09.434
can merge it into a single MOE.

01:29:09.835 --> 01:29:11.615
By keeping each FFN from

01:29:11.915 --> 01:29:14.095
different different data owners as an MOE.

01:29:14.335 --> 01:29:16.454
And we don't have to do anything with about

01:29:16.455 --> 01:29:18.835
the shared layers because they're already synchronized.

01:29:19.505 --> 01:29:21.585
They're already identical to the public model.

01:29:21.585 --> 01:29:23.925
So they already kind of know how to work together.

01:29:25.845 --> 01:29:27.925
So this is good. Now we haven't talked

01:29:27.925 --> 01:29:30.055
about the routers. So what do we do with the

01:29:30.195 --> 01:29:32.314
router? So router is essentially

01:29:32.314 --> 01:29:34.554
an N Way classifier. It involves a

01:29:34.555 --> 01:29:36.624
made matrix that is n by d, where n

01:29:36.624 --> 01:29:38.624
is a number of experts and d

01:29:38.625 --> 01:29:40.865
is hidden dimension. So what we can

01:29:40.865 --> 01:29:43.035
do is to decompose this

01:29:43.035 --> 01:29:44.575
router matrix into

01:29:45.055 --> 01:29:46.915
n number of d dimensional

01:29:47.555 --> 01:29:49.715
embeddings, which we call router embeddings. And

01:29:49.715 --> 01:29:51.755
each router embedding correspond to each

01:29:52.315 --> 01:29:54.555
expert. So we have our pop that correspond

01:29:54.555 --> 01:29:56.584
to the public FFN, r one,

01:29:56.585 --> 01:29:57.605
r two, and r three.

01:29:58.645 --> 01:30:01.065
So where do they come from? They can come from

01:30:01.485 --> 01:30:03.725
each data owner's training. When it's data

01:30:03.725 --> 01:30:05.665
owner does their own training, they

01:30:05.785 --> 01:30:07.805
have this MOE with two experts.

01:30:07.805 --> 01:30:10.044
That means they were going to have two router

01:30:10.044 --> 01:30:11.915
embeddings. They can use

01:30:12.135 --> 01:30:14.225
domain embedding from their data. So they

01:30:14.225 --> 01:30:16.385
have our pop, which is a domain embedding

01:30:16.385 --> 01:30:17.965
for a public model. Public

01:30:18.905 --> 01:30:21.044
data that is frozen And then

01:30:21.044 --> 01:30:23.245
there is R1, which is a domain embedding

01:30:23.245 --> 01:30:25.624
for their data We can keep it trainable.

01:30:25.985 --> 01:30:28.084
So when they do their training, it's essentially

01:30:28.465 --> 01:30:30.594
still keeping the public model as

01:30:30.595 --> 01:30:32.664
the anchor model. But trained two

01:30:32.665 --> 01:30:34.865
additional component, FFN and

01:30:34.865 --> 01:30:37.164
r one. And once that training

01:30:37.164 --> 01:30:39.245
is done, they contribute this R1

01:30:39.245 --> 01:30:41.255
to the public space game. We

01:30:41.255 --> 01:30:43.605
can concatenate all the router embeddings from

01:30:44.175 --> 01:30:46.413
every data owner and form it a

01:30:46.414 --> 01:30:48.635
single and that can be readily

01:30:48.635 --> 01:30:49.615
available as a router.

01:30:50.815 --> 01:30:52.905
So that's kinda how it works. So just

01:30:53.145 --> 01:30:55.225
putting things together, we start

01:30:55.225 --> 01:30:57.385
with the stance model that is public

01:30:57.520 --> 01:30:59.835
model. Then each data owner takes

01:30:59.835 --> 01:31:01.825
it, and add a second

01:31:01.965 --> 01:31:04.125
FFN as well as router embedding to do their

01:31:04.125 --> 01:31:06.225
own training. Using the

01:31:06.225 --> 01:31:08.684
public model as an anchor model. And

01:31:08.684 --> 01:31:10.945
then after their training is done, they contribute

01:31:11.945 --> 01:31:14.104
FFN as router embedding. And we can make it

01:31:14.104 --> 01:31:16.195
as a single mixture

01:31:16.195 --> 01:31:18.155
of expert model that is read a little bit

01:31:18.795 --> 01:31:20.865
by doing combination, We

01:31:20.865 --> 01:31:22.965
keep each separate. We concatenate

01:31:23.104 --> 01:31:25.215
all the router embeddings to make it an

01:31:25.215 --> 01:31:27.245
NWE. And again, this is exactly

01:31:27.625 --> 01:31:29.235
identical to the standard MOE RTT

01:31:29.875 --> 01:31:31.435
so we can reuse a lot of things.

01:31:32.475 --> 01:31:34.885
Now As I briefly hinted earlier,

01:31:35.025 --> 01:31:37.265
because it's training as model alert, that means

01:31:37.265 --> 01:31:38.915
opt in and opt out is very

01:31:39.295 --> 01:31:41.615
easy. So for instance, let's say one of our

01:31:41.615 --> 01:31:43.645
data owners is New York Times,

01:31:44.125 --> 01:31:46.285
They used to participate, but they wanna opt out

01:31:46.285 --> 01:31:48.455
later on. Then we can simply do

01:31:48.455 --> 01:31:50.394
that by removing the parameters

01:31:50.535 --> 01:31:52.655
contributed by neural times.

01:31:52.815 --> 01:31:54.975
And it's guaranteed that none

01:31:54.975 --> 01:31:57.015
of the rest of the parameter is using

01:31:57.015 --> 01:31:58.515
your times data in any way.

01:32:01.155 --> 01:32:03.495
Cool. Okay. So that was a model. Now

01:32:03.495 --> 01:32:05.035
let's move on to experiments.

01:32:06.365 --> 01:32:08.445
So designing experimental setup for

01:32:08.445 --> 01:32:10.695
this work was very hard because we need

01:32:10.695 --> 01:32:12.935
some public data and some private

01:32:12.935 --> 01:32:15.115
data sets, proprietary data sets.

01:32:15.805 --> 01:32:18.135
Those private data sets are by definition private,

01:32:18.135 --> 01:32:20.225
so we don't have access to them. But

01:32:20.225 --> 01:32:22.725
we still wanted to simulate the most realistic

01:32:23.025 --> 01:32:25.205
scenario as much as possible.

01:32:25.345 --> 01:32:27.415
So we came up with some setup.

01:32:27.415 --> 01:32:29.575
So we have public data that is common

01:32:29.575 --> 01:32:32.075
crawl. This is an Internet data that is actually

01:32:32.085 --> 01:32:34.535
public. And then we define

01:32:34.755 --> 01:32:36.984
seven data sets that we consider to

01:32:36.984 --> 01:32:37.644
be proprietary.

01:32:39.325 --> 01:32:41.405
One thing that is kind of nice is

01:32:41.405 --> 01:32:43.455
actually three of them are

01:32:43.455 --> 01:32:45.715
actual proprietary datasets. They used

01:32:45.775 --> 01:32:47.845
to be public, that's why we still had

01:32:47.845 --> 01:32:49.545
them downloaded on our

01:32:49.995 --> 01:32:52.095
our computer. But now data owners

01:32:52.555 --> 01:32:54.604
close their data, so it's no longer

01:32:54.605 --> 01:32:56.705
public. It's proprietary. So we

01:32:57.265 --> 01:32:59.675
use those datasets. The other

01:32:59.975 --> 01:33:02.394
four datasets are public, such simulated

01:33:02.535 --> 01:33:04.545
proprietary data sets, but we also

01:33:04.545 --> 01:33:06.565
chose domains in a way that they

01:33:06.625 --> 01:33:08.755
reflect realistic scenarios. So

01:33:08.755 --> 01:33:10.834
for instance, we included code because

01:33:10.835 --> 01:33:12.835
corporate code is representative

01:33:13.130 --> 01:33:14.665
case. Included

01:33:15.205 --> 01:33:17.355
papers because a lot of publishers actually

01:33:17.355 --> 01:33:19.565
don't release the data

01:33:19.565 --> 01:33:20.494
for public use.

01:33:22.414 --> 01:33:24.554
Okay. And these are some training details. I'm

01:33:24.555 --> 01:33:26.534
not going to go over all the details, but

01:33:26.615 --> 01:33:28.695
just to highlight a few, we trained our

01:33:28.695 --> 01:33:30.794
7,000,000,000 parametric model.

01:33:31.085 --> 01:33:32.545
We trained from scratch

01:33:33.164 --> 01:33:35.235
because we wanted to make sure the base

01:33:35.235 --> 01:33:37.255
model is guaranteed to only train

01:33:37.475 --> 01:33:39.515
on public data. But if

01:33:39.515 --> 01:33:41.754
you take any open weight model, it's

01:33:41.755 --> 01:33:43.785
hard to guarantee that. So we had to

01:33:43.845 --> 01:33:46.085
train a model ourselves. So we'd pre

01:33:46.085 --> 01:33:48.025
train a model from scratch

01:33:48.265 --> 01:33:50.605
on public data for 1,000,000,000,000 tokens.

01:33:51.065 --> 01:33:53.305
And then we assume each of

01:33:53.305 --> 01:33:55.505
the seven data owners

01:33:55.505 --> 01:33:57.045
does continual pretraining

01:33:58.055 --> 01:33:59.765
over 50,000,000,000 tokens each.

01:34:00.745 --> 01:34:02.975
So that's the setup. Here are the

01:34:02.975 --> 01:34:05.315
results. So in the paper, we included

01:34:05.374 --> 01:34:07.065
over 30 different

01:34:07.405 --> 01:34:09.504
benchmarks. Here, I'm just going to show four

01:34:09.504 --> 01:34:11.825
numbers. Two of them

01:34:11.825 --> 01:34:14.085
are generic general purpose benchmarks.

01:34:14.435 --> 01:34:16.215
Like MML Pro and AGILE.

01:34:17.125 --> 01:34:19.365
Also have a domain specific benchmark on

01:34:19.365 --> 01:34:21.495
math domain. This math two

01:34:21.495 --> 01:34:23.595
is actually the average of two math benchmarks.

01:34:24.005 --> 01:34:26.165
And then we also have an average of 30

01:34:26.165 --> 01:34:28.275
benchmarks. This

01:34:28.275 --> 01:34:30.595
is a public model that is trained on public

01:34:30.595 --> 01:34:31.985
data only. So

01:34:32.705 --> 01:34:34.484
in some sense, this is strictly

01:34:34.945 --> 01:34:36.895
our baseline. Because if you

01:34:37.135 --> 01:34:39.244
don't have our thing, then this will be

01:34:39.245 --> 01:34:41.084
the only model we would have access to.

01:34:41.325 --> 01:34:43.355
Right? And then we

01:34:43.355 --> 01:34:44.975
also compare with specialized

01:34:45.435 --> 01:34:47.215
experts. That means the

01:34:47.675 --> 01:34:49.705
data that is trained on

01:34:50.265 --> 01:34:51.964
local private data, but individually.

01:34:52.655 --> 01:34:54.814
Not altogether, for each of the

01:34:54.815 --> 01:34:56.875
domain data. We included three of them.

01:34:57.515 --> 01:34:59.615
And what we found is when benchmarks

01:34:59.915 --> 01:35:01.715
are in distribution to those

01:35:01.955 --> 01:35:04.034
data owners' data, then it helps a

01:35:04.035 --> 01:35:06.135
lot. For instance, you can see the math

01:35:06.195 --> 01:35:08.505
expert getting a really good performance on

01:35:08.505 --> 01:35:10.765
math benchmarks. However, when benchmarks

01:35:10.985 --> 01:35:13.154
are not in domain, when it's out

01:35:13.155 --> 01:35:15.234
of domain, then the performance can be much

01:35:15.234 --> 01:35:17.265
worse. So So on average,

01:35:17.265 --> 01:35:19.034
the performance gain is not much.

01:35:21.035 --> 01:35:23.115
Then we also compare with prior

01:35:23.115 --> 01:35:25.215
model merging base lines, including

01:35:25.215 --> 01:35:27.185
model soup with those parameter

01:35:27.260 --> 01:35:29.625
averaging. BTM, which does unsungpling,

01:35:30.655 --> 01:35:31.955
and BTX which uses MOE.

01:35:32.955 --> 01:35:35.095
So what we found is first of

01:35:35.095 --> 01:35:37.354
all, overall, model margin kind of

01:35:37.354 --> 01:35:39.434
helps. If you look at the average numbers, there are

01:35:39.434 --> 01:35:41.524
always better than the public model,

01:35:41.525 --> 01:35:43.605
so it means it's kind of promising, but

01:35:43.605 --> 01:35:45.615
not as much. And

01:35:45.615 --> 01:35:47.795
in particular, if you look at the bars

01:35:47.915 --> 01:35:50.075
for the math benchmarks, we can

01:35:50.075 --> 01:35:51.595
see that even though the

01:35:52.235 --> 01:35:54.315
data the model frame of math data had

01:35:54.315 --> 01:35:56.539
a very good performance gain,

01:35:57.595 --> 01:36:00.095
when they were combined with other experts, the performers

01:36:00.515 --> 01:36:02.754
dropped a lot. So that means, even

01:36:02.755 --> 01:36:04.545
though model merging kind of helps

01:36:05.185 --> 01:36:07.605
when you are merging multiple models,

01:36:08.145 --> 01:36:09.605
You're kinda losing the benefit

01:36:10.305 --> 01:36:12.334
that was that was obtained by

01:36:12.334 --> 01:36:13.635
its individual expert.

01:36:14.774 --> 01:36:16.825
So it's kind of not as satisfying.

01:36:18.104 --> 01:36:20.265
And finally, here is our model, which is

01:36:20.265 --> 01:36:22.315
called FlexOLMOM. And is

01:36:22.315 --> 01:36:24.655
actually getting a very good performance. It's outperforming

01:36:24.795 --> 01:36:26.864
all the models. And in particular, if you

01:36:26.865 --> 01:36:29.075
look at the math benchmark, it can

01:36:29.075 --> 01:36:31.155
retain the full benefit coming

01:36:31.155 --> 01:36:33.385
from training on math data, which is kinda

01:36:33.385 --> 01:36:35.624
exciting. We also have a pretty

01:36:35.624 --> 01:36:37.945
good benefit on generic

01:36:38.085 --> 01:36:40.005
benchmark like MMLU Pro and AI developed,

01:36:40.595 --> 01:36:42.904
is likely because those benchmarks contain

01:36:43.065 --> 01:36:45.164
a lot of subsets coming from different

01:36:45.305 --> 01:36:46.605
domains, and therefore,

01:36:47.365 --> 01:36:48.585
our models that can combine,

01:36:50.050 --> 01:36:52.434
datasets coming from diverse domains

01:36:52.434 --> 01:36:54.675
kind of help. So on average, we're getting

01:36:54.675 --> 01:36:56.445
about 41 relative gain.

01:36:57.085 --> 01:36:58.305
Over the public model.

01:37:00.064 --> 01:37:02.144
What I found personally more exciting

01:37:02.144 --> 01:37:04.234
is we actually also compare with an

01:37:04.235 --> 01:37:06.335
upper bound model that is called unrestricted

01:37:06.475 --> 01:37:08.385
MOE. This means we upcycle

01:37:08.684 --> 01:37:10.705
our public model to make it on MOE.

01:37:10.785 --> 01:37:13.025
And then we assume we have

01:37:13.025 --> 01:37:15.084
joint access across all the

01:37:15.084 --> 01:37:17.144
datasets and do joint training across

01:37:17.144 --> 01:37:19.225
all of them. So this is on upper bound

01:37:19.225 --> 01:37:21.465
because this is, like, actually

01:37:21.465 --> 01:37:23.625
not permitted in our case, but we just wanted to

01:37:23.625 --> 01:37:25.635
estimate the upper bound. And of course,

01:37:25.635 --> 01:37:27.715
this upper bound is better than all the models

01:37:27.715 --> 01:37:30.144
we have because tune training is

01:37:30.144 --> 01:37:32.463
indeed very helpful. But our model

01:37:32.464 --> 01:37:34.475
is actually close. We're actually

01:37:34.475 --> 01:37:36.555
retaining over 90% of the

01:37:36.555 --> 01:37:38.175
benefit of full training

01:37:39.335 --> 01:37:41.655
This means, if you are willing to pay

01:37:41.655 --> 01:37:43.850
for 10% of performance gains,

01:37:44.025 --> 01:37:46.075
then you can get a lot of very

01:37:46.075 --> 01:37:48.495
exciting functionalities such as modular

01:37:48.555 --> 01:37:50.485
training, keeping the data

01:37:50.625 --> 01:37:52.785
private, and being able to obtain and

01:37:52.785 --> 01:37:54.385
opt out almost for free.

01:37:56.335 --> 01:37:58.574
And finally, we have kind of like

01:37:58.575 --> 01:38:00.845
nice opt out experiments. So here, the dark

01:38:01.405 --> 01:38:03.565
bar is our combined model with

01:38:03.565 --> 01:38:05.595
eight x and then the

01:38:05.595 --> 01:38:07.794
lighter one is when we opt out one of

01:38:07.794 --> 01:38:09.875
the experts coming from use So

01:38:09.875 --> 01:38:11.855
what we see is, of course, on benchmark

01:38:11.915 --> 01:38:14.085
related to news, we are getting performance drop,

01:38:14.085 --> 01:38:16.505
which is kind of expected. But for

01:38:16.645 --> 01:38:19.145
other datasets, we are getting a similar result.

01:38:19.415 --> 01:38:21.495
That means even if you opt out one of the

01:38:21.495 --> 01:38:23.745
data owners it overall have minimal

01:38:23.745 --> 01:38:25.925
impact especially to other domains.

01:38:28.475 --> 01:38:30.485
Cool. So, this is the

01:38:30.485 --> 01:38:32.535
it. So I will just

01:38:32.535 --> 01:38:34.105
wrap up with a few takeaways.

01:38:34.665 --> 01:38:36.845
So in the beginning of the talk, I motivated

01:38:36.985 --> 01:38:39.075
this challenge of data running out.

01:38:39.235 --> 01:38:41.334
And then said, oh, it might not be the quantities.

01:38:42.365 --> 01:38:44.865
Insufficient. It might be data sets are fragmented.

01:38:45.595 --> 01:38:47.605
They're owned by different entities and cannot be

01:38:47.605 --> 01:38:49.745
shared. So we need a new

01:38:49.745 --> 01:38:52.225
training recipe that can benefit from this fragmented

01:38:52.225 --> 01:38:54.305
data. So we

01:38:54.545 --> 01:38:56.624
our proposal was to leverage the

01:38:56.624 --> 01:38:58.564
modular architecture of MOE

01:38:59.065 --> 01:39:01.305
but then modify the training algorithm

01:39:01.305 --> 01:39:03.195
to benefit from fragmented data set.

01:39:03.515 --> 01:39:04.585
And in particular, we

01:39:06.135 --> 01:39:08.374
have different experts, different FFMs

01:39:08.374 --> 01:39:10.365
to be trained independently by each

01:39:10.845 --> 01:39:12.785
data owner completely in isolation,

01:39:13.085 --> 01:39:15.105
and then they can contribute parameters

01:39:15.405 --> 01:39:17.425
and we can combine them into a single

01:39:17.485 --> 01:39:19.614
MoE model. And there were a few technical

01:39:19.615 --> 01:39:22.015
challenges. I had to make it MOE aware,

01:39:22.015 --> 01:39:23.895
so we came up with some in

01:39:24.315 --> 01:39:26.485
and we were training and nonparametric model.

01:39:27.605 --> 01:39:29.465
And our results show that it outperforms

01:39:29.765 --> 01:39:31.874
training on public data only, It

01:39:31.875 --> 01:39:33.955
approaches during training on all data sets,

01:39:33.955 --> 01:39:36.084
which is an upper bound, and provide

01:39:36.084 --> 01:39:37.925
free opt out, and also in.

01:39:39.205 --> 01:39:41.525
Yeah. So I don't think the problem is solved,

01:39:41.525 --> 01:39:43.695
of course, from this single work,

01:39:43.695 --> 01:39:46.115
and there are many open problems to be solved.

01:39:46.255 --> 01:39:47.865
I'll just talk about a

01:39:48.505 --> 01:39:50.585
few commonly asked questions when

01:39:50.585 --> 01:39:52.595
I shared this work. So one

01:39:52.595 --> 01:39:54.695
of the common last question is does it work

01:39:54.695 --> 01:39:56.504
for post training? Our answer used

01:39:56.745 --> 01:39:59.145
to be no because this was all about pre training.

01:39:59.145 --> 01:40:01.605
But very recently, in fact, earlier

01:40:01.985 --> 01:40:04.065
this week, we released a new preprint

01:40:04.125 --> 01:40:06.175
that shows that, oh, it works

01:40:06.175 --> 01:40:08.474
for post training to some degree. You need to motivate

01:40:08.715 --> 01:40:10.814
the recipe a little bit, but with some modification

01:40:10.955 --> 01:40:12.575
it works, so check it out.

01:40:13.295 --> 01:40:15.615
The other problem that is actually a bigger

01:40:15.615 --> 01:40:17.665
problem is inference cost. So you

01:40:18.255 --> 01:40:20.695
might have realized earlier already,

01:40:20.995 --> 01:40:23.245
but as you have an additional

01:40:23.245 --> 01:40:25.315
data owner, you need all whole new

01:40:25.315 --> 01:40:27.414
FFN And because FFN

01:40:27.414 --> 01:40:29.575
is a majority of parameters, it means

01:40:29.575 --> 01:40:31.655
the inference cost can grow very

01:40:31.655 --> 01:40:33.675
quickly. We don't want that. So

01:40:33.675 --> 01:40:36.115
can we make it more efficient?

01:40:36.335 --> 01:40:38.725
And in particular, can we do more fine grained

01:40:39.124 --> 01:40:41.365
distributed learning so that growing can be a little

01:40:41.365 --> 01:40:43.524
more incremental? Just need a little

01:40:43.525 --> 01:40:45.934
longer discussion I think the short

01:40:45.935 --> 01:40:48.425
answer is Yeah.

01:40:48.485 --> 01:40:50.285
Short answer is yes conceptually,

01:40:51.185 --> 01:40:53.265
but this first require having a

01:40:53.265 --> 01:40:55.394
base model that is an MOE In

01:40:55.394 --> 01:40:57.795
particular, this MOE has to be fine grained,

01:40:57.795 --> 01:40:59.955
and it also has to be modular, meaning

01:40:59.955 --> 01:41:02.434
it expert is already kind of specialized

01:41:02.435 --> 01:41:04.404
in terms of domains rather than

01:41:04.645 --> 01:41:06.805
superficial lexical cues, which

01:41:06.805 --> 01:41:08.585
we don't really have right now.

01:41:09.145 --> 01:41:11.385
We are working on it, and in fact, I'm giving

01:41:11.385 --> 01:41:13.465
another talk at another workshop

01:41:13.465 --> 01:41:15.605
tomorrow on this, like, motor and weight

01:41:16.645 --> 01:41:18.265
topic. So if you're interested in, please check it out.

01:41:18.725 --> 01:41:20.584
Yeah. That's it. Thank you for listening.

01:41:25.225 --> 01:41:27.305
Yeah. Thanks, Si Wang. If you have any

01:41:27.305 --> 01:41:28.975
questions, feel free to come to the

01:41:29.374 --> 01:41:29.674
middle.

01:41:39.925 --> 01:41:41.315
Hi. Thanks for a great talk.

01:41:42.195 --> 01:41:44.445
Have one question about the datas.

01:41:44.445 --> 01:41:45.135
It's not sure.

01:41:49.555 --> 01:41:51.774
Can you hear me? Oh, yes. Yeah. So

01:41:51.775 --> 01:41:53.934
I have one question about the data sizes

01:41:53.935 --> 01:41:55.845
that you're using for the different experts.

01:41:56.005 --> 01:41:58.124
So I think all of them were trained on the same amount

01:41:58.124 --> 01:41:59.385
of data, but in practice,

01:42:00.125 --> 01:42:02.285
some domains might have a lot, some might be very

01:42:02.285 --> 01:42:04.544
scarce. Do you have any results on, like,

01:42:04.785 --> 01:42:06.815
how much imbalance can this method handle?

01:42:07.465 --> 01:42:09.325
Yeah. That's a very great question.

01:42:09.545 --> 01:42:11.704
Oh, yeah. We train all experts

01:42:11.705 --> 01:42:13.855
on the 50,000,000,000 to tokens. In

01:42:13.855 --> 01:42:16.015
fact, we have some domains where the data set

01:42:16.015 --> 01:42:18.225
side was smaller, but in that case, we just repeat

01:42:18.225 --> 01:42:20.285
it. But I think it can be smaller than

01:42:20.285 --> 01:42:22.405
that, so I think it's an important question.

01:42:22.725 --> 01:42:25.225
We didn't have answers to that in this work.

01:42:25.285 --> 01:42:27.075
In fact, I think that is very relevant

01:42:27.315 --> 01:42:29.555
to the fine grained MOE that I

01:42:29.555 --> 01:42:31.945
mentioned because if data owner with very

01:42:31.945 --> 01:42:34.055
small data still wanna participate,

01:42:34.195 --> 01:42:36.434
then that means each expert has to be a little

01:42:36.434 --> 01:42:37.975
small, meaning you need to have a

01:42:38.535 --> 01:42:40.625
finer grain MOE. And,

01:42:41.005 --> 01:42:43.185
the second question I have is around, like,

01:42:43.425 --> 01:42:45.045
when you train these experts,

01:42:45.765 --> 01:42:47.854
on private data, but then eventually

01:42:47.854 --> 01:42:49.615
release them to the general public,

01:42:50.255 --> 01:42:52.535
there's threats around, like, could people do

01:42:52.535 --> 01:42:54.854
in membership inference attacks or data extraction

01:42:54.854 --> 01:42:56.425
attacks and get out private data?

01:42:56.985 --> 01:42:59.235
Do you have any thoughts on how likely are they

01:42:59.235 --> 01:43:01.075
or do they not work at all in

01:43:01.294 --> 01:43:03.385
this setup? Yeah. I think that's a very great question.

01:43:03.385 --> 01:43:05.184
We also got many questions.

01:43:05.625 --> 01:43:07.445
So yes, there is a risk

01:43:07.685 --> 01:43:09.845
that even if you don't share the raw data, if

01:43:09.845 --> 01:43:11.755
you're sharing model parameters, people

01:43:12.075 --> 01:43:14.155
can do membership inference or extraction

01:43:14.155 --> 01:43:16.095
attack to extract some data.

01:43:16.635 --> 01:43:18.895
We empirically validated, and I think this

01:43:18.955 --> 01:43:20.955
is in consistent this is

01:43:21.095 --> 01:43:22.995
consistent with literature

01:43:23.135 --> 01:43:24.915
that when we try to do that, we can

01:43:25.539 --> 01:43:27.734
identify it's possible to extract

01:43:27.735 --> 01:43:29.765
less than 1% of the data, but not more

01:43:30.005 --> 01:43:32.164
than that, meaning they're very small amount.

01:43:32.164 --> 01:43:34.374
But I think this risk is real. What

01:43:34.375 --> 01:43:36.425
I would say is if your

01:43:36.425 --> 01:43:38.755
data I think there can be many meanings of

01:43:39.075 --> 01:43:41.155
private data. If your data is private

01:43:41.155 --> 01:43:42.215
because it's sensitive,

01:43:43.245 --> 01:43:45.355
meaning even 1% of extracted

01:43:45.575 --> 01:43:47.654
data can be risky, then I think you

01:43:47.655 --> 01:43:49.275
maybe shouldn't use this approach.

01:43:49.715 --> 01:43:51.954
Because still sharing the parameter is

01:43:51.955 --> 01:43:54.225
problem. However, if your data is private,

01:43:54.445 --> 01:43:56.145
because the data is valuable,

01:43:56.705 --> 01:43:58.745
then I think that means,

01:43:58.745 --> 01:44:00.804
oh, if we're constructing 1% of

01:44:00.805 --> 01:44:02.964
data doesn't really matter, so in that case you can

01:44:02.964 --> 01:44:04.965
use that. So I think we can use this

01:44:05.205 --> 01:44:07.025
approach mainly for the latter case.

01:44:08.145 --> 01:44:09.855
Makes sense. Thanks a lot for the talk.

01:44:12.415 --> 01:44:14.815
Thank you very much for your talk. I had a question

01:44:14.815 --> 01:44:17.044
about in part from

01:44:17.044 --> 01:44:19.284
this mathematical dataset that you showed that

01:44:19.285 --> 01:44:21.385
there is a big benefit

01:44:21.385 --> 01:44:23.435
for them, But

01:44:23.595 --> 01:44:25.455
if you were the owner of these datasets,

01:44:26.525 --> 01:44:28.705
that is very specialized and high valued

01:44:29.035 --> 01:44:31.333
for your problem. There is no benefit

01:44:31.334 --> 01:44:33.644
for them to share So I see why

01:44:33.644 --> 01:44:35.745
the others are gaining, but

01:44:35.805 --> 01:44:38.085
this actor is not gaining

01:44:38.085 --> 01:44:40.225
anything. So what do you think about

01:44:40.225 --> 01:44:42.374
this kind of value proposition

01:44:42.374 --> 01:44:44.465
for them? Why would they bring their

01:44:44.465 --> 01:44:46.775
dataset in? Yeah. I think that's a really

01:44:47.255 --> 01:44:49.755
great question as well because this incentive problem is a

01:44:49.815 --> 01:44:51.885
real issue. I think there could be two main

01:44:51.885 --> 01:44:54.044
scenarios that they might wanna share. One

01:44:54.045 --> 01:44:56.225
is they do want to

01:44:56.225 --> 01:44:58.295
collaborate with other data owners. First, they

01:44:58.295 --> 01:45:00.535
may have good data math data, but they don't have

01:45:00.535 --> 01:45:02.714
good good data. Maybe there's another data

01:45:02.714 --> 01:45:04.755
owner that have good good data, but not

01:45:04.755 --> 01:45:07.195
math. If they just

01:45:07.495 --> 01:45:09.655
alone, they can't just do their work, but if they

01:45:09.655 --> 01:45:11.894
collaborate, they can do a better job. So I think it can

01:45:11.894 --> 01:45:13.925
be a way to collaborate with other

01:45:13.925 --> 01:45:16.085
entities. The second scenario

01:45:16.085 --> 01:45:18.355
which is a little less tech technically is I

01:45:18.375 --> 01:45:20.725
think this can be a new

01:45:20.865 --> 01:45:23.025
avenue for data licensing or even data

01:45:23.025 --> 01:45:25.155
market. Data market is

01:45:25.155 --> 01:45:27.495
already a thing. People purchase the data

01:45:28.765 --> 01:45:29.825
by giving

01:45:31.205 --> 01:45:33.254
yeah, People purchase the

01:45:33.255 --> 01:45:35.335
