02:05:27.145 --> 02:05:29.325
Hi, everyone. So, please

02:05:30.055 --> 02:05:32.215
come back. We will start for our

02:05:32.215 --> 02:05:32.645
first

02:05:34.575 --> 02:05:35.385
oral talk.

02:06:03.205 --> 02:06:05.425
Alright. So, thanks for,

02:06:06.895 --> 02:06:08.675
coming, for the, the workshops.

02:06:09.135 --> 02:06:10.765
The second session, in

02:06:11.245 --> 02:06:13.184
the this morning is, oral presentations.

02:06:14.905 --> 02:06:17.064
So the first oral presentation will be

02:06:17.064 --> 02:06:19.225
by, May, who'll

02:06:19.225 --> 02:06:20.605
introduce, omics.

02:06:22.825 --> 02:06:24.285
Let's welcome, May.

02:06:36.955 --> 02:06:38.805
Hello? Oh, okay. There we go.

02:06:39.925 --> 02:06:42.084
Okay. Hi, everyone. My name is Mei Yi,

02:06:42.084 --> 02:06:43.705
and I'll be presenting OMICS,

02:06:44.095 --> 02:06:46.255
which is a data mixing framework I worked on

02:06:46.255 --> 02:06:48.195
while I was interning at AI2.

02:06:49.955 --> 02:06:51.725
So in data mixing, you have

02:06:51.965 --> 02:06:54.044
many different pretraining domains. So here

02:06:54.044 --> 02:06:56.285
we have web math

02:06:56.425 --> 02:06:58.444
and code. And your goal is to figure out how to

02:06:58.445 --> 02:07:00.514
combine training data from all of them, such

02:07:01.155 --> 02:07:03.125
that your model exhibits a broad variety

02:07:03.665 --> 02:07:05.874
of capabilities. Currently,

02:07:06.255 --> 02:07:08.735
the naive approach is just brute force

02:07:08.735 --> 02:07:10.695
search or tuning the ratios manually.

02:07:11.075 --> 02:07:13.314
So this requires a bunch of trial and error,

02:07:13.315 --> 02:07:15.434
which is but there

02:07:15.435 --> 02:07:17.595
are also many recent mixing methods that

02:07:17.595 --> 02:07:19.455
learn data mixes more efficiently.

02:07:21.365 --> 02:07:23.514
Today, rather than talking about a new

02:07:23.995 --> 02:07:26.234
mixing method, I'm going to focus on issues

02:07:26.235 --> 02:07:28.455
that arise in data mixing during model

02:07:29.015 --> 02:07:31.094
development. So consider this timeline

02:07:31.095 --> 02:07:32.925
here, so you're on the left,

02:07:33.325 --> 02:07:35.565
maybe a few months or weeks out from the day

02:07:35.565 --> 02:07:37.895
you want to start pre training your big model,

02:07:38.215 --> 02:07:40.374
And you have your domains and you want to figure

02:07:40.375 --> 02:07:41.595
out the data mix.

02:07:43.155 --> 02:07:45.244
So So the first question is how do I

02:07:45.245 --> 02:07:47.305
get a good mix on these domains

02:07:47.305 --> 02:07:49.624
that I start out with? And it's not

02:07:49.624 --> 02:07:51.795
super clear what to do, so should you run

02:07:51.795 --> 02:07:53.954
some existing mixing method out

02:07:53.955 --> 02:07:56.414
of the box? Should you build your own method?

02:07:56.585 --> 02:07:58.665
And so that's the first challenge, and this is

02:07:58.665 --> 02:08:00.704
sort of what we faced at the start of

02:08:00.705 --> 02:08:02.305
building the Omo three model.

02:08:04.064 --> 02:08:06.215
So Second, let's suppose we figured out the

02:08:06.215 --> 02:08:08.664
initial mix and next challenge

02:08:08.725 --> 02:08:10.504
is that data is constantly

02:08:10.915 --> 02:08:13.075
changing throughout model development. And

02:08:13.075 --> 02:08:15.335
this is just reality, so people are

02:08:15.664 --> 02:08:17.825
constantly building new data sets, or they

02:08:17.825 --> 02:08:20.074
might be refining some other data sets,

02:08:20.155 --> 02:08:22.575
and so the question becomes how do you maintain

02:08:22.795 --> 02:08:24.655
a good mixture throughout development?

02:08:26.535 --> 02:08:28.695
So let's get into the first part of

02:08:28.695 --> 02:08:30.794
the talk, As some background on the data

02:08:30.795 --> 02:08:32.225
mixing problem, we have

02:08:32.865 --> 02:08:34.874
m training domains, d one through d

02:08:34.874 --> 02:08:36.705
m, and we want to train a model of

02:08:37.375 --> 02:08:39.075
s parameters on r tokens.

02:08:39.635 --> 02:08:41.815
And the data mix p is a probability

02:08:41.875 --> 02:08:43.735
vector over the m domains

02:08:44.605 --> 02:08:46.845
and the final mixed training dataset is created by

02:08:46.845 --> 02:08:48.935
sampling r times p

02:08:48.935 --> 02:08:51.275
of I tokens for each domain d I.

02:08:52.005 --> 02:08:54.163
We then denote l m of r

02:08:54.164 --> 02:08:56.245
p as a target model trained according

02:08:56.245 --> 02:08:58.445
to data p. And we evaluate

02:08:58.445 --> 02:09:00.525
this trained model on end tasks

02:09:00.525 --> 02:09:02.735
where we measure bits per byte also

02:09:02.735 --> 02:09:05.195
known as BPB, so lower is better here.

02:09:05.755 --> 02:09:07.835
And the goal in data mixing is to

02:09:07.835 --> 02:09:09.915
efficiently find the mix that minimizes

02:09:10.215 --> 02:09:12.295
the average BPB over all the

02:09:12.295 --> 02:09:13.355
downstream tasks.

02:09:15.075 --> 02:09:17.235
So there are many mixing methods that aim

02:09:17.235 --> 02:09:19.374
to solve this problem I just described, and

02:09:19.374 --> 02:09:21.535
a lot of them actually follow this general template

02:09:21.535 --> 02:09:23.155
which we call offline mixing

02:09:23.770 --> 02:09:25.965
schema. The first step is we train

02:09:25.965 --> 02:09:28.224
a bunch of smaller models, and we

02:09:28.225 --> 02:09:30.405
call these proxy models on different mixes,

02:09:30.625 --> 02:09:32.955
and we then evaluate each of models

02:09:33.175 --> 02:09:35.215
on the tasks we care about. So we're

02:09:35.375 --> 02:09:37.455
refer to this as a swarm of proxy

02:09:37.455 --> 02:09:39.855
models. Next,

02:09:39.855 --> 02:09:42.095
using the swarm, we're going to fit a

02:09:42.095 --> 02:09:44.175
regression model that learns the relationship between

02:09:44.175 --> 02:09:46.035
the mixing ratios and the performance.

02:09:46.745 --> 02:09:48.845
This regression model, this f hat

02:09:48.905 --> 02:09:51.405
of p, is going to allow us to predict the performance

02:09:51.465 --> 02:09:52.765
of any data mixture.

02:09:54.015 --> 02:09:56.035
Finally, we propose a mix by

02:09:56.195 --> 02:09:58.354
solving an optimization problem that uses the

02:09:58.354 --> 02:10:00.675
regression model as a surrogate for true down

02:10:01.155 --> 02:10:03.395
performance. Basically, we find the mix that

02:10:03.395 --> 02:10:04.934
has the best predictive performance.

02:10:05.825 --> 02:10:07.845
These are the three steps of the offline

02:10:07.905 --> 02:10:10.084
mixing schema, and so the question is,

02:10:10.275 --> 02:10:12.434
how do we construct a good mixing

02:10:12.434 --> 02:10:14.455
method from from this general procedure?

02:10:16.215 --> 02:10:18.375
So by examining this schema, we came

02:10:18.375 --> 02:10:20.434
up with a list of questions that a

02:10:20.435 --> 02:10:22.775
practitioner needs to go through in order to instantiate

02:10:22.915 --> 02:10:25.014
a mixing method. For the sake of

02:10:25.015 --> 02:10:27.333
time, I'll only be presenting the three bolded

02:10:27.334 --> 02:10:29.605
questions, So first, in constructing

02:10:29.665 --> 02:10:31.834
the swarm, you need to determine how

02:10:31.835 --> 02:10:34.075
big these proxy models are and how many

02:10:34.075 --> 02:10:36.345
of them you need. And then for the regression

02:10:36.345 --> 02:10:38.605
fitting, the obvious question is what regression

02:10:38.745 --> 02:10:40.865
model to use. You can also take a look

02:10:40.865 --> 02:10:43.104
at our paper to see how we investigate a bunch

02:10:43.105 --> 02:10:44.325
of other design choices.

02:10:45.775 --> 02:10:48.015
To study each design choice, our target

02:10:48.015 --> 02:10:50.044
models are 1,000,000,000 parameters

02:10:50.044 --> 02:10:51.825
trained on 100,000,000,000 tokens,

02:10:52.215 --> 02:10:54.294
and we experimented on DCLM baseline,

02:10:54.295 --> 02:10:56.475
a web corpus that was partitioned into

02:10:56.755 --> 02:10:58.914
24 topic based domains. So

02:10:58.914 --> 02:11:01.095
mixing on things like literature, politics,

02:11:01.805 --> 02:11:03.985
science, and so on. And we measured BPB

02:11:04.124 --> 02:11:06.474
bits per byte on a suite of 52

02:11:06.475 --> 02:11:08.814
tasks that covered math, code, and question

02:11:09.035 --> 02:11:11.414
answering. The first question

02:11:11.475 --> 02:11:13.775
is proxy model size. How

02:11:13.775 --> 02:11:15.934
small can these proxy models be so that

02:11:15.934 --> 02:11:17.995
they still provide meaningful signal for

02:11:17.995 --> 02:11:20.125
the 1b target models? To

02:11:20.125 --> 02:11:22.445
study this, we took a bunch of mixture ratios,

02:11:22.445 --> 02:11:24.865
and for each mix, we trained both a proxy

02:11:24.865 --> 02:11:27.095
model and a target model on it. And

02:11:27.095 --> 02:11:29.175
then we looked at the performance rankings of

02:11:29.175 --> 02:11:31.205
these mixtures at both scales and

02:11:31.205 --> 02:11:33.315
measured the rank correlation. Basically,

02:11:33.315 --> 02:11:35.475
we're trying to see if the data mixtures that

02:11:35.475 --> 02:11:37.485
did the best on small models also

02:11:37.485 --> 02:11:39.605
do the best on large models. And we

02:11:39.605 --> 02:11:41.845
repeat this experiment for many different proxy

02:11:41.845 --> 02:11:42.585
model sizes.

02:11:43.895 --> 02:11:45.915
So Here are our results. So on the x

02:11:45.915 --> 02:11:47.495
axis is the size of the

02:11:47.975 --> 02:11:50.055
models. We did one, 15, 30,

02:11:50.055 --> 02:11:52.135
and 60,000,000. And on the

02:11:52.135 --> 02:11:54.295
y axis is the rank correlation with our target

02:11:54.295 --> 02:11:56.525
one b parameter model. We

02:11:56.525 --> 02:11:58.945
see that everything over 15,000,000 parameters

02:11:59.005 --> 02:12:00.545
has pretty high rank correlation.

02:12:01.045 --> 02:12:03.205
So our recommendation is that at least to

02:12:03.205 --> 02:12:05.565
do well at the 1b scale, we recommend

02:12:05.565 --> 02:12:07.914
using proxy models that are over

02:12:07.915 --> 02:12:09.195
15,000,000 parameters.

02:12:10.315 --> 02:12:12.505
So next question is how big

02:12:12.505 --> 02:12:14.584
the swarm should be. So concretely, if we

02:12:14.584 --> 02:12:16.485
want to mix over m

02:12:16.624 --> 02:12:19.124
domains, how many proxy models do we need?

02:12:19.705 --> 02:12:21.784
For our experiment, we construct various

02:12:21.785 --> 02:12:23.815
swarms by sweeping over m, the

02:12:23.815 --> 02:12:25.995
number of domains, and k, the

02:12:25.995 --> 02:12:28.075
number of proxy runs. And we measure

02:12:28.075 --> 02:12:30.395
performance of the resulting mix relative

02:12:30.395 --> 02:12:31.855
to the best mix per m.

02:12:33.295 --> 02:12:35.145
This figure here shows our results

02:12:35.545 --> 02:12:38.045
So on the x axis is the swarm size.

02:12:38.385 --> 02:12:40.805
Where we've scaled everything not to be the absolute

02:12:40.865 --> 02:12:42.945
size but just size relative to the number

02:12:42.945 --> 02:12:45.005
of domains. And on the

02:12:45.005 --> 02:12:47.084
y axis is the error, so how close

02:12:47.085 --> 02:12:49.075
we are to the best mix we found.

02:12:49.555 --> 02:12:51.795
And the thing to notice here is that across

02:12:51.795 --> 02:12:53.905
all of the m's, from six

02:12:54.285 --> 02:12:56.525
to 24 domains, the curves all look

02:12:56.525 --> 02:12:58.875
pretty much the same. So this indicates

02:12:59.015 --> 02:13:01.174
that error is only dependent on the

02:13:01.175 --> 02:13:03.185
swarm size relative to the to the

02:13:03.185 --> 02:13:05.474
number of domains. Basically, this means

02:13:05.475 --> 02:13:07.954
that we just need O of M proxy models

02:13:07.955 --> 02:13:09.755
to learn a good mix over

02:13:10.055 --> 02:13:12.305
M domains. The last

02:13:12.305 --> 02:13:14.384
question here is what regression model

02:13:14.385 --> 02:13:16.205
should we use to capture the relationship

02:13:16.505 --> 02:13:18.365
between the mix and downstream performance?

02:13:19.415 --> 02:13:21.655
Study this, we take a swarm and try five different

02:13:21.655 --> 02:13:23.785
regression models on it. These,

02:13:24.345 --> 02:13:26.684
range from gradient boosting to Gaussian processes

02:13:26.825 --> 02:13:28.045
to parametric functions.

02:13:30.035 --> 02:13:31.955
We measure the regression fit of each

02:13:32.515 --> 02:13:34.214
model on held out mixes.

02:13:34.995 --> 02:13:37.305
And we looked at how this depends on swarm size. So

02:13:37.305 --> 02:13:39.465
this reveals something pretty interesting, which is

02:13:39.465 --> 02:13:41.485
that different regression models need

02:13:41.735 --> 02:13:43.975
different amounts of data, which is something that hasn't

02:13:43.975 --> 02:13:45.955
been exposed in prior works.

02:13:46.434 --> 02:13:48.615
So for instance, this method, bimix,

02:13:48.675 --> 02:13:50.354
is quite good for small swarms.

02:13:50.835 --> 02:13:53.235
While light GBM needs much more proxy

02:13:53.235 --> 02:13:55.355
runs. And to figure out which one we

02:13:55.355 --> 02:13:57.825
use, we noticed that the log linear

02:13:57.825 --> 02:14:00.025
models, which are in purple, outperform

02:14:00.085 --> 02:14:02.165
the other models consistently once you

02:14:02.165 --> 02:14:04.445
have at least 75 runs.

02:14:04.985 --> 02:14:07.175
Basically at least three times the number of

02:14:07.175 --> 02:14:09.235
domains. Also saw that

02:14:09.235 --> 02:14:11.655
Log linear outperforms other models in downstream

02:14:12.115 --> 02:14:14.135
performance. So our conclusion from this study

02:14:14.135 --> 02:14:16.525
was to use the log linear regression model.

02:14:17.965 --> 02:14:20.144
And after studying all these design choices,

02:14:20.145 --> 02:14:22.225
we used our findings to define a

02:14:22.225 --> 02:14:23.605
base method, Olmec space,

02:14:24.395 --> 02:14:26.475
our recommended default configuration of the offline

02:14:26.475 --> 02:14:28.805
mixing schema. So all the text

02:14:29.184 --> 02:14:31.424
in pink are the modifications to the schema

02:14:31.425 --> 02:14:33.315
based on our empirical findings.

02:14:33.555 --> 02:14:36.115
So in this warm step, we use 30,000,000

02:14:36.115 --> 02:14:38.144
parameter proxy models Basically,

02:14:38.145 --> 02:14:40.305
anything above 15 is fine, and

02:14:40.305 --> 02:14:42.085
we use o of m proxy models.

02:14:42.684 --> 02:14:44.765
And in the regression model step, we fit

02:14:44.765 --> 02:14:46.835
a log linear model for each task.

02:14:47.275 --> 02:14:49.434
And finally, in the optimization step,

02:14:49.434 --> 02:14:51.665
I wasn't able to go over this due

02:14:51.665 --> 02:14:53.685
to time, but we added a constraint

02:14:54.225 --> 02:14:56.395
to ensure that proposed mixes don't

02:14:59.715 --> 02:15:01.814
extend data mixing to the data constraint

02:15:02.035 --> 02:15:04.425
setting. So

02:15:04.425 --> 02:15:06.624
moving into this question of how to

02:15:06.625 --> 02:15:08.685
mix under evolving data. So

02:15:08.685 --> 02:15:10.925
this is a pretty novel problem setting,

02:15:10.925 --> 02:15:12.865
but we were motivated by what we saw in

02:15:13.184 --> 02:15:15.264
practice, which is that data is consistently being

02:15:15.265 --> 02:15:16.885
updated throughout model development.

02:15:17.655 --> 02:15:19.815
So consider the following scenario.

02:15:19.815 --> 02:15:22.035
You and, your team are training a

02:15:22.434 --> 02:15:24.515
LLM and you have a pretty good mix

02:15:24.515 --> 02:15:26.675
over your data sets and you spent some time

02:15:26.995 --> 02:15:29.024
figuring out these ratios, but

02:15:29.025 --> 02:15:31.295
then the data changes. So maybe

02:15:31.295 --> 02:15:33.375
someone on your team finishes preparing a new data

02:15:33.375 --> 02:15:35.335
set that is just so incredibly

02:15:35.715 --> 02:15:37.874
good that you have to add it in, or you realize

02:15:37.875 --> 02:15:39.025
that you should more aggressive

02:15:42.735 --> 02:15:44.925
to be made. Happen and the

02:15:44.925 --> 02:15:46.655
question is what do you do with this mix

02:15:47.295 --> 02:15:49.245
that you've prepared on your old data sets?

02:15:50.845 --> 02:15:52.925
I'll formally describe the problem of mixing as

02:15:52.925 --> 02:15:54.945
data evolves now. So we start

02:15:54.945 --> 02:15:57.210
with a domain set d of m

02:15:57.211 --> 02:15:59.293
domains, then this is updated into

02:15:59.294 --> 02:16:01.535
d prime, which consists of m prime

02:16:01.535 --> 02:16:03.544
domains. Based on our

02:16:03.544 --> 02:16:05.624
experience, we found four major ways that

02:16:05.624 --> 02:16:07.564
D is updated into D prime.

02:16:07.835 --> 02:16:10.075
We have addition and removal of datasets.

02:16:10.075 --> 02:16:11.535
So these are pretty self explanatory.

