And therefore, we want to flatten the distribution

02:54:29.985 --> 02:54:31.525
flatten the head of the distribution.

02:54:32.616 --> 02:54:34.775
So ideally, what we want is an

02:54:34.775 --> 02:54:36.684
data selection which looks like this.

02:54:36.845 --> 02:54:38.626
You cut off the tail of the distribution,

02:54:39.625 --> 02:54:41.715
and you flatten the head. You make

02:54:41.955 --> 02:54:44.285
you try to achieve roughly uniform

02:54:44.285 --> 02:54:46.545
distribution over just the right number of facts.

02:54:51.965 --> 02:54:54.205
And then we can, of course, do this in the synthetic data

02:54:54.205 --> 02:54:56.225
setting when we actually control

02:54:56.285 --> 02:54:58.525
the data distribution. We can actually make this change, but

02:54:58.525 --> 02:55:00.674
that's not very useful. If you want to really deploy

02:55:00.674 --> 02:55:01.725
this in the real world,

02:55:04.555 --> 02:55:06.855
so what we show in this work is

02:55:07.415 --> 02:55:09.675
that there is a simple proxy that we can use

02:55:09.955 --> 02:55:11.655
for the frequency of the items.

02:55:12.424 --> 02:55:14.435
Which is the loss. So

02:55:14.435 --> 02:55:16.755
if we measure the loss

02:55:16.755 --> 02:55:18.695
on the fact tokens,

02:55:19.875 --> 02:55:22.134
after, say, a small number of iterations.

02:55:26.575 --> 02:55:28.585
For tokens in the tail, the

02:55:28.585 --> 02:55:30.965
fact loss is high because they haven't been memorized at all.

02:55:31.125 --> 02:55:33.450
For tokens in the head, the fact loss

02:55:33.450 --> 02:55:35.575
is the the loss on

02:55:35.575 --> 02:55:37.735
those tokens is, is lower because

02:55:37.735 --> 02:55:39.885
the model has memorized them already to some

02:55:39.885 --> 02:55:42.014
extent. So after doing one epoch,

02:55:42.014 --> 02:55:43.425
we can use,

02:55:44.395 --> 02:55:46.894
the current loss do the selection.

02:55:48.725 --> 02:55:51.046
We basically choose a threshold for facts

02:55:51.046 --> 02:55:53.125
which are above that threshold. We think of those

02:55:53.125 --> 02:55:55.246
as facts in the tail. We just drop

02:55:55.246 --> 02:55:57.256
those facts completely from the

02:55:57.256 --> 02:55:57.925
training set.

02:55:59.555 --> 02:56:01.625
And for facts that whose loss is below

02:56:01.625 --> 02:56:04.125
the threshold, we keep them with probability proportional

02:56:04.184 --> 02:56:06.185
to the loss. Lower loss means we have a

02:56:06.185 --> 02:56:08.335
lower probability of keeping them because

02:56:08.335 --> 02:56:09.745
they are likely in the head already.

02:56:11.825 --> 02:56:13.894
So and in practice,

02:56:13.955 --> 02:56:16.095
this does leak to significantly better

02:56:16.095 --> 02:56:18.235
memorization. So on these synthetic

02:56:18.235 --> 02:56:20.655
datasets, our algorithm

02:56:21.525 --> 02:56:23.865
kind of improves the capacity. It doesn't drop.

02:56:24.115 --> 02:56:26.195
As you see on the left. And on the right, you

02:56:26.195 --> 02:56:28.545
see when we have a a power law distribution,

02:56:28.685 --> 02:56:30.305
the capacity increases significantly.

02:56:34.376 --> 02:56:36.455
So this is this is great. So we have a training

02:56:36.455 --> 02:56:38.684
method which is just based on loss.

02:56:38.765 --> 02:56:40.925
Is something you can implement without trying

02:56:40.925 --> 02:56:42.865
to learn anything about the data distribution.

02:56:45.174 --> 02:56:47.435
And it gives us significantly better memorization.

02:56:51.315 --> 02:56:53.635
But in this current form, this model still needs

02:56:53.635 --> 02:56:55.715
a couple of things. So first, it needs fact

02:56:55.715 --> 02:56:57.945
boundaries. I guess to be able

02:56:58.085 --> 02:57:00.205
to compute the laws on effect,

02:57:00.395 --> 02:57:02.475
I need to know where the fact begins and

02:57:02.475 --> 02:57:04.715
ends. And while I can do

02:57:04.715 --> 02:57:06.875
that for synthetic data, for real data, this may be

02:57:06.875 --> 02:57:08.915
harder. Thankfully,

02:57:09.054 --> 02:57:11.134
recent work has shown that you can

02:57:11.134 --> 02:57:12.115
actually annotate

02:57:13.385 --> 02:57:15.666
data using using language models.

02:57:16.515 --> 02:57:18.735
To to define fact boundaries around

02:57:18.735 --> 02:57:20.515
facts. So I guess this worked by Joe Vital,

02:57:22.255 --> 02:57:24.755
released this dataset where they just took Wikipedia

02:57:25.355 --> 02:57:27.835
and used an LLM to, to annotate

02:57:28.055 --> 02:57:30.155
some of the sentences as facts.

02:57:30.695 --> 02:57:32.975
With clear boundaries. So that's what

02:57:32.975 --> 02:57:34.635
we use in our, real world experiments.

02:57:37.215 --> 02:57:39.375
We need to modify the selection method

02:57:39.375 --> 02:57:41.565
a little bit because now we have both fact tokens

02:57:41.565 --> 02:57:43.625
and non fact tokens. The non fact

02:57:43.625 --> 02:57:45.425
tokens, we want to leave them as is.

02:57:45.746 --> 02:57:47.955
For the fact tokens, we want to essentially

02:57:47.955 --> 02:57:49.285
apply our method, which is what we do.

02:57:53.474 --> 02:57:55.554
The other thing our method needs to know is how

02:57:55.554 --> 02:57:57.665
much capacity there is, and, how

02:57:57.665 --> 02:57:59.925
much entropy there is in each

02:58:00.594 --> 02:58:02.915
each fact. This is something that we don't

02:58:02.915 --> 02:58:03.955
really know because,

02:58:04.995 --> 02:58:07.075
we don't really know how much entropy each fact

02:58:07.075 --> 02:58:09.344
has. So we just tune

02:58:09.344 --> 02:58:11.775
this parameter tau, to,

02:58:12.065 --> 02:58:14.305
to figure out what the best

02:58:14.305 --> 02:58:15.095
value is.

02:58:21.455 --> 02:58:23.695
So we did this on this real world dataset

02:58:23.935 --> 02:58:26.435
from Wikipedia. Here are some results.

02:58:30.086 --> 02:58:32.166
Our selection method, if you apply it,

02:58:32.405 --> 02:58:34.594
it significantly improves the

02:58:34.594 --> 02:58:36.755
fact accuracy, the number of facts that the model

02:58:36.755 --> 02:58:38.655
can memorize. So

02:58:38.875 --> 02:58:40.955
I guess, you can see

02:58:40.955 --> 02:58:43.044
this plot in two ways. You can either think of

02:58:43.045 --> 02:58:45.205
this as we're getting 30%

02:58:45.205 --> 02:58:46.255
more facts

02:58:47.295 --> 02:58:48.434
for the same capacity

02:58:50.005 --> 02:58:52.085
or I guess maybe a more flattering way to think of

02:58:52.085 --> 02:58:54.086
this is if you train on the full

02:58:54.086 --> 02:58:56.285
dataset, you would need a 10 times larger dataset

02:58:56.895 --> 02:58:59.295
10 times larger model to memorize

02:58:59.295 --> 02:59:00.295
the same number of facts.

02:59:02.725 --> 02:59:04.916
So with our selection, we can

02:59:04.916 --> 02:59:07.155
get the same amount of fact memorization with

02:59:07.155 --> 02:59:09.395
the 10 times smaller model.

02:59:09.405 --> 02:59:11.765
Compared to training. The full

02:59:11.765 --> 02:59:13.805
dataset. Now you might worry

02:59:13.805 --> 02:59:16.305
that, we are distorting the data the training

02:59:16.365 --> 02:59:18.444
data distribution that may hurt accuracy

02:59:18.445 --> 02:59:20.585
on other tasks on language understanding.

02:59:21.435 --> 02:59:23.666
So we did some ablation

02:59:23.965 --> 02:59:26.135
studies here and we see that

02:59:26.135 --> 02:59:28.385
the accuracy on various, natural

02:59:28.385 --> 02:59:30.805
language understanding task is essentially unaffected.

02:59:31.095 --> 02:59:32.795
By our selection method.

02:59:37.095 --> 02:59:39.335
Get similar results for fine tuning on

02:59:39.335 --> 02:59:41.115
a semi synthetic authors

02:59:41.555 --> 02:59:43.604
and, paper

02:59:43.665 --> 02:59:45.285
titles, dataset.

02:59:47.116 --> 02:59:49.344
Once again, we can while full

02:59:49.344 --> 02:59:50.715
dataset training just

02:59:51.354 --> 02:59:52.725
does not do very well,

02:59:54.325 --> 02:59:56.206
our method consistently,

02:59:57.235 --> 02:59:58.275
improves over,

03:00:00.424 --> 03:00:02.854
over the original model. Without affecting

03:00:02.915 --> 03:00:03.974
the general capability.

