04:59:43.661 --> 04:59:45.850
and a little bit of background, there's

04:59:45.850 --> 04:59:47.711
a lot of excitement these days

04:59:48.090 --> 04:59:50.101
around AI agents specially

04:59:50.322 --> 04:59:52.391
designed for the digital world. We can

04:59:52.770 --> 04:59:55.171
see them in coding assistance, we see them in computer

04:59:55.171 --> 04:59:57.591
use agents, and even frameworks like

04:59:57.752 --> 05:00:00.072
open cloud are creating a lot of interesting,

05:00:00.473 --> 05:00:02.791
discussions around the world. But we

05:00:02.791 --> 05:00:05.231
must remember that our most

05:00:05.690 --> 05:00:07.821
meaningful activities still happen in person.

05:00:08.381 --> 05:00:10.631
In the physical world. In fact, we're here today

05:00:10.911 --> 05:00:12.991
in Brazil attending

05:00:12.991 --> 05:00:15.111
this conference as an example of that.

05:00:15.832 --> 05:00:17.881
So that's the reason why I'm

05:00:18.822 --> 05:00:20.831
thinking about our research work and how it

05:00:21.231 --> 05:00:23.311
relates to the idea of agentic ambient

05:00:23.311 --> 05:00:25.331
intelligence. Building AI technology

05:00:25.931 --> 05:00:28.172
that can augment and super power our

05:00:28.172 --> 05:00:29.871
physical in person activities.

05:00:30.681 --> 05:00:32.841
And to illustrate this vision, I want to show

05:00:32.841 --> 05:00:34.931
two examples. First,

05:00:34.992 --> 05:00:36.931
think about wearable smart glasses.

05:00:37.531 --> 05:00:39.311
Imagine this user that's assembling

05:00:39.691 --> 05:00:41.791
a complex dresser, and we

05:00:41.791 --> 05:00:43.891
want to move beyond glasses that simply

05:00:43.951 --> 05:00:46.252
record what's happening and rather

05:00:46.252 --> 05:00:48.584
we want agents that understand the

05:00:48.584 --> 05:00:50.877
task so deeply that they can

05:00:50.877 --> 05:00:53.171
provide real time feedback and guidance

05:00:53.171 --> 05:00:54.121
to to the user.

05:00:55.241 --> 05:00:57.261
Second, think of, high stress

05:00:57.912 --> 05:01:00.332
residential move where this human,

05:01:01.191 --> 05:01:03.221
has a robot and he's acting

05:01:03.221 --> 05:01:05.381
as a director deciding what to keep,

05:01:05.381 --> 05:01:07.441
what to donate, what to pack. While

05:01:07.441 --> 05:01:09.781
the robot acts as an expert assistance

05:01:10.891 --> 05:01:12.151
that is doing all the

05:01:13.190 --> 05:01:15.211
boring tedious labor work of of packing.

05:01:16.191 --> 05:01:18.211
So for that, the robot needs to understand

05:01:18.271 --> 05:01:20.511
ambiguous commands to understand

05:01:20.572 --> 05:01:22.611
environmental context, to be

05:01:22.611 --> 05:01:24.871
able to do that effectively. So to achieve this

05:01:24.871 --> 05:01:27.151
type of vision, we need to bridge

05:01:27.151 --> 05:01:29.261
a gap between what

05:01:29.261 --> 05:01:31.281
we can do in digital reasoning,

05:01:31.981 --> 05:01:34.131
and physical action. So today, I

05:01:34.131 --> 05:01:36.071
wanna cover four core capabilities

05:01:36.591 --> 05:01:38.752
that, we think are required to make this,

05:01:38.992 --> 05:01:41.260
a reality. So let me start with

05:01:41.260 --> 05:01:43.121
a more specific example. Imagine

05:01:43.340 --> 05:01:45.221
that our smart glass user

05:01:45.381 --> 05:01:47.381
is assembling this drawer, and

05:01:47.441 --> 05:01:49.771
they point a finger at a specific board

05:01:50.011 --> 05:01:52.332
on the floor. And they may ask, should this

05:01:52.332 --> 05:01:54.412
board be installed horizontally like the

05:01:54.412 --> 05:01:55.361
one just finished?

05:01:56.721 --> 05:01:58.181
Or maybe in the case of the robot,

05:01:58.801 --> 05:02:01.031
the the mover asks, the robot, can

05:02:01.031 --> 05:02:02.791
you wrap up wrap this cup?

05:02:03.351 --> 05:02:05.591
Like, the around and the ones I washed five minutes

05:02:05.591 --> 05:02:07.841
ago? So

05:02:08.002 --> 05:02:10.242
that brings us to our first capability that I'll

05:02:10.242 --> 05:02:12.279
talk about today which is about the ability

05:02:12.279 --> 05:02:14.680
of vision language models to answer

05:02:14.680 --> 05:02:16.690
questions with space

05:02:16.690 --> 05:02:18.931
time preferences. So

05:02:19.172 --> 05:02:21.252
if we look at current models, they actually fail at

05:02:21.252 --> 05:02:23.491
this. They struggle with this fine grained

05:02:23.491 --> 05:02:25.701
special to polar reasoning especially

05:02:25.701 --> 05:02:27.291
when the queries themselves

05:02:27.851 --> 05:02:30.311
like this one, rely on time based

05:02:31.350 --> 05:02:33.451
event references for temporal anchoring

05:02:34.511 --> 05:02:36.531
or gesture cues for spatial anchoring.

05:02:37.731 --> 05:02:39.961
So why is this happening?

05:02:40.101 --> 05:02:42.422
Perhaps one of the issues as we see today in

05:02:42.422 --> 05:02:44.631
in most foundation models is that

05:02:44.791 --> 05:02:47.111
potentially there is not enough data in the training to capture

05:02:47.111 --> 05:02:48.871
this type of of capability.

05:02:49.171 --> 05:02:51.491
So we try to look at that and try to bridge

05:02:51.491 --> 05:02:53.601
that gap by introducing this framework which we

05:02:53.601 --> 05:02:55.902
call Strefer, It's a data

05:02:56.201 --> 05:02:58.531
generation engine that automatically synthesizes

05:02:58.832 --> 05:03:01.252
data training data for these particular scenarios.

05:03:02.041 --> 05:03:04.171
So idea is that by learning with

05:03:04.171 --> 05:03:05.871
this automatically generated data,

05:03:06.822 --> 05:03:08.961
then we can enable new

05:03:08.961 --> 05:03:11.331
critical capabilities. So I'm gonna show you a couple.

05:03:11.891 --> 05:03:14.051
So here's an example. We can using this

05:03:14.051 --> 05:03:16.301
data, we generate automatically. We can make

05:03:16.621 --> 05:03:18.911
models learn to interpret queries

05:03:19.471 --> 05:03:21.651
where a user is pointing at an entity.

05:03:22.231 --> 05:03:24.391
For example, here, the user points at a person

05:03:24.391 --> 05:03:26.411
in the video and may ask something like,

05:03:26.651 --> 05:03:28.110
could you describe his action?

05:03:28.701 --> 05:03:30.941
And in the prompt, the model not only

05:03:30.941 --> 05:03:32.721
receives the question, but also receives

05:03:33.201 --> 05:03:35.541
a mask that's derived from the pointing gesture

05:03:36.381 --> 05:03:38.081
to help it help the model clarify

05:03:38.481 --> 05:03:39.571
who he is.

05:03:40.610 --> 05:03:42.731
So So so using this data we

05:03:42.731 --> 05:03:44.811
we created, we can get the models to answer

05:03:44.811 --> 05:03:46.841
this this type of question. So this is

05:03:46.841 --> 05:03:49.261
spatial referencing. But another interesting

05:03:50.091 --> 05:03:52.252
capability is that of temporal references. So

05:03:52.252 --> 05:03:54.261
here, we can ask questions such

05:03:54.261 --> 05:03:56.551
as what is happening to that boy

05:03:56.551 --> 05:03:59.051
wearing glasses from three seconds to nine seconds.

05:03:59.582 --> 05:04:01.822
So again, temporarily, pointing

05:04:01.822 --> 05:04:03.291
at certain parts of the video.

05:04:03.951 --> 05:04:06.031
And now that even the this boy wearing

05:04:06.031 --> 05:04:08.371
glasses is not visible during that time span,

05:04:08.821 --> 05:04:10.893
of the video. So we with this

05:04:10.893 --> 05:04:13.051
data generation pipeline, we can also generate

05:04:13.051 --> 05:04:14.511
these type of negative questions

05:04:15.231 --> 05:04:17.471
asking about identities that aren't

05:04:17.471 --> 05:04:19.571
even there in that portion of the video.

05:04:19.891 --> 05:04:22.051
And this is just to make sure that the model isn't

05:04:22.051 --> 05:04:24.231
just guessing or hallucinating information or taking

05:04:24.231 --> 05:04:26.371
any shortcuts, but really truly

05:04:26.371 --> 05:04:27.840
understanding the video's timeline.

05:04:29.371 --> 05:04:31.771
But of course, we can combine these two types of referencing.

05:04:31.771 --> 05:04:33.781
Right? We can ask models to

05:04:34.341 --> 05:04:36.821
or we can also hopefully enable models to interpret

05:04:36.821 --> 05:04:38.860
this more complex questions

05:04:38.860 --> 05:04:41.020
that involve both mask

05:04:41.020 --> 05:04:42.401
and timestamp preferences.

05:04:43.531 --> 05:04:45.590
So here's here for example, we we can

05:04:45.590 --> 05:04:47.850
ask what did he do, what in seconds, and the models

05:04:48.172 --> 05:04:50.271
hopefully get able to to answer that type of questions.

05:04:50.332 --> 05:04:52.412
So of course, the key problem is how do we generate

05:04:52.412 --> 05:04:54.411
this type data? And

05:04:55.021 --> 05:04:57.261
and here's the full straightforward data generation pipeline.

05:04:57.261 --> 05:04:59.601
It's a complex pipeline, but I would say

05:04:59.601 --> 05:05:01.141
before talking about how it works is that

05:05:02.180 --> 05:05:04.411
this idea of I want to get a new

05:05:04.411 --> 05:05:06.701
capability my models are not good at it,

05:05:06.941 --> 05:05:09.101
So how can I reuse what I already

05:05:09.101 --> 05:05:11.211
can do to generate data so I can

05:05:11.211 --> 05:05:13.002
train my model to get to that view capability?

05:05:13.351 --> 05:05:15.371
So this is what we're trying to do here.

05:05:15.431 --> 05:05:17.111
And we orchestrate this,

05:05:18.281 --> 05:05:20.221
multiple models that have simpler

05:05:21.271 --> 05:05:23.431
complementary strengths, and in

05:05:23.431 --> 05:05:25.449
our implementation, we focus just on

05:05:25.449 --> 05:05:27.521
using open models to achieve

05:05:27.521 --> 05:05:29.561
this. So here's how

05:05:29.561 --> 05:05:31.801
level how it works. Given an input video,

05:05:32.121 --> 05:05:34.521
we first use a video LLM to describe

05:05:34.521 --> 05:05:36.511
the active subjects in the video like

05:05:36.752 --> 05:05:38.902
the girl in a white dress, and then

05:05:38.902 --> 05:05:40.441
use an NLLM to describe

05:05:41.951 --> 05:05:44.031
to to parse this into noun

05:05:44.031 --> 05:05:46.131
phrases and and generalized nouns.

05:05:46.451 --> 05:05:48.611
Which then we can feed into a novel module

05:05:48.611 --> 05:05:50.791
that produces masculine. These are

05:05:52.161 --> 05:05:54.441
tracked object segmentation masks So, this

05:05:54.441 --> 05:05:56.861
give us a way to follow the entities throughout

05:05:57.001 --> 05:05:58.541
the the video sequence.

05:05:59.091 --> 05:06:01.172
So that's one way to generate the special

05:06:01.172 --> 05:06:03.441
references and then on the other hand, we

05:06:03.441 --> 05:06:05.601
have the temporal references which

05:06:05.601 --> 05:06:07.141
which we can generate by

05:06:08.082 --> 05:06:10.551
dividing the video into short clips, and

05:06:10.611 --> 05:06:12.851
then using a video alarm to describe

05:06:12.851 --> 05:06:14.641
the short clips especially

05:06:14.861 --> 05:06:16.691
focusing on behavior style

05:06:17.332 --> 05:06:19.360
descriptions for all the entities that

05:06:19.360 --> 05:06:21.601
we had discovered in the upper portion.

05:06:22.162 --> 05:06:24.502
And finally, now we have this set of temporally

05:06:24.563 --> 05:06:26.588
dense transcripts for all the clips

05:06:26.588 --> 05:06:28.860
in the video and all the entities with

05:06:28.860 --> 05:06:31.039
tracks so we can use that to generate

05:06:33.011 --> 05:06:35.001
instruction style question answer in pairs.

05:06:35.801 --> 05:06:38.301
Okay? So we can run this and because it's fully automatic,

05:06:38.791 --> 05:06:41.152
we can do this at scale. So here's an

05:06:41.152 --> 05:06:42.861
example of what we can obtain.

05:06:43.931 --> 05:06:46.011
Where we have input videos, we run through

05:06:46.011 --> 05:06:48.057
our software pipeline, can generate

05:06:48.057 --> 05:06:50.091
this type of instructions which

05:06:50.091 --> 05:06:52.561
include a segmentation mask and a question,

05:06:53.100 --> 05:06:55.440
using this space and temporal references

05:06:55.711 --> 05:06:58.131
together with the response that we need the model to produce.

05:06:59.270 --> 05:07:01.751
Okay? So as I said, because this is completely automatic,

05:07:01.751 --> 05:07:03.901
we can scale it up. And in fact,

05:07:04.041 --> 05:07:06.381
using this pipeline, we generated nearly a million

05:07:06.491 --> 05:07:08.510
data samples from just over

05:07:08.651 --> 05:07:10.350
4,000 source videos

05:07:11.822 --> 05:07:14.322
from from the next QA dataset. So

05:07:14.631 --> 05:07:16.711
so now we have this large data. What can we use this

05:07:16.711 --> 05:07:19.121
to train a model? Again, state models are

05:07:19.361 --> 05:07:21.521
to get them to do new things. Many times,

05:07:21.521 --> 05:07:23.551
we need to to train them with large amount of

05:07:23.551 --> 05:07:25.631
data, so we did that and we trained

