05:31:06.881 --> 05:31:09.082
and widely circulated novel that

05:31:09.082 --> 05:31:11.162
it has, intentionally or not, appeared

05:31:11.162 --> 05:31:13.332
many times in several common pretraining

05:31:13.332 --> 05:31:15.381
data sets. Because it was published

05:31:15.381 --> 05:31:17.331
in 1954 and still remains

05:31:17.571 --> 05:31:19.350
under copyright in many jurisdictions,

05:31:19.901 --> 05:31:21.981
it serves as a useful example of

05:31:21.981 --> 05:31:24.471
how protected material can make its way into

05:31:24.471 --> 05:31:25.331
training corpora.

05:31:26.529 --> 05:31:28.949
So why exactly is this a problem?

05:31:29.681 --> 05:31:31.922
Language models can memorize parts of their

05:31:31.922 --> 05:31:34.021
training data and later reproduce them verbatim

05:31:34.231 --> 05:31:36.520
inference time. This is really concerning

05:31:36.520 --> 05:31:38.678
for works protected by copyright, as they

05:31:38.678 --> 05:31:40.821
should not be reproduced in an unauthorized

05:31:40.821 --> 05:31:42.861
manner. So above is just one example,

05:31:43.181 --> 05:31:45.326
Given the opening sentence of the Lord of the

05:31:45.326 --> 05:31:47.471
Rings, the model can easily continue with the

05:31:47.471 --> 05:31:48.371
exact continuation.

05:31:49.811 --> 05:31:52.211
When language models memorize and reproduce

05:31:52.211 --> 05:31:54.281
copyright protected data, this can

05:31:54.281 --> 05:31:56.201
actually pose several harms to,

05:31:56.441 --> 05:31:58.681
several groups. So on one side, data

05:31:58.681 --> 05:32:00.921
creators so like authors, artists,

05:32:00.921 --> 05:32:03.041
etcetera, whose creative works are

05:32:03.041 --> 05:32:04.742
scraped and used for model training,

05:32:05.582 --> 05:32:07.981
often did not authorize such usage in the first

05:32:07.981 --> 05:32:10.121
place. And are also commonly neither

05:32:10.121 --> 05:32:12.281
properly compensated nor credited for the

05:32:12.281 --> 05:32:14.733
use of their work. And one current

05:32:14.951 --> 05:32:17.271
economic argument is that AI generated creative

05:32:17.271 --> 05:32:19.291
work may actually depress demand for some

05:32:19.291 --> 05:32:21.471
forms of human creative labor by

05:32:21.531 --> 05:32:23.471
crowding out human creative works in the market.

05:32:24.031 --> 05:32:26.371
The second problem is for model developers

05:32:27.111 --> 05:32:29.451
who may also potentially face legal liabilities

05:32:29.601 --> 05:32:31.761
if their models are determined to have infringed

05:32:31.761 --> 05:32:33.871
on copyright. Now this is indeed

05:32:33.871 --> 05:32:36.031
a bit speculative, given the current

05:32:36.031 --> 05:32:38.371
ambiguity of the legal landscape surrounding

05:32:38.430 --> 05:32:40.440
this issue, but in recent years,

05:32:40.440 --> 05:32:42.680
this has led to several model or dataset

05:32:42.680 --> 05:32:44.760
takedowns and now as well as

05:32:44.760 --> 05:32:46.701
many ongoing legal court cases.

05:32:47.211 --> 05:32:49.291
And I'm not going to get too much

05:32:49.291 --> 05:32:51.591
into the legal side of things, but

05:32:51.591 --> 05:32:53.912
we can see here that the current legal landscape

05:32:53.912 --> 05:32:55.711
surrounding generative AI and

05:32:56.591 --> 05:32:58.619
potential copyright infringement is currently very

05:32:58.619 --> 05:32:59.921
contentious and unresolved.

05:33:00.631 --> 05:33:02.821
So we can see, that as of April

05:33:02.821 --> 05:33:04.850
2026, and this is from a fairly

05:33:04.850 --> 05:33:07.090
up to date online tracker, there are currently

05:33:07.090 --> 05:33:09.551
many active law suits surrounding this

05:33:09.691 --> 05:33:11.651
issue, both in The United States

05:33:12.451 --> 05:33:14.631
as well as globally across the entire

05:33:14.850 --> 05:33:17.141
world. And all this to say, the attempt

05:33:17.141 --> 05:33:19.221
to curb the reproduction of copyrighted

05:33:19.221 --> 05:33:21.001
works and language models,

05:33:21.241 --> 05:33:23.261
is in the best interest of both parties.

05:33:23.502 --> 05:33:25.921
So both data creators and model developers.

05:33:26.561 --> 05:33:27.940
Of this ongoing debate.

05:33:28.931 --> 05:33:30.951
And just before I go into describing

05:33:31.091 --> 05:33:33.350
our work, I also want to discuss

05:33:33.411 --> 05:33:35.190
some naive solutions to this problem.

05:33:35.502 --> 05:33:37.582
So the cleanest way to minimize the

05:33:37.582 --> 05:33:39.721
reproduction of copyrighted works

05:33:39.961 --> 05:33:42.361
is to remove the high risk data from your pretraining

05:33:42.361 --> 05:33:44.301
corpus and retrain your language model.

05:33:44.521 --> 05:33:46.681
Such that it never sees sensitive data in the

05:33:46.681 --> 05:33:49.021
first place. However, as we all know,

05:33:49.261 --> 05:33:50.821
training can be really costly,

05:33:51.381 --> 05:33:53.461
and training a model from scratch is already an

05:33:53.461 --> 05:33:55.473
expensive endeavor and it's even more

05:33:55.473 --> 05:33:57.791
of an uphill battle to retrain all the existing

05:33:57.791 --> 05:33:59.851
models that may have already been exposed.

05:34:00.492 --> 05:34:02.721
Copyrighted protected material. Another

05:34:02.860 --> 05:34:04.321
issue is kind of more practical.

05:34:05.261 --> 05:34:07.742
Copyrighted data also tends to be higher quality

05:34:07.742 --> 05:34:09.811
relative to other data sources.

05:34:09.971 --> 05:34:12.231
So including them wholesale is also suboptimal

05:34:12.531 --> 05:34:13.991
for downstream model performance.

05:34:15.501 --> 05:34:17.901
So the question that our work seeks to address

05:34:17.901 --> 05:34:19.971
today is, how can we elicit

05:34:20.190 --> 05:34:22.270
output in a post hoc manner from existing

05:34:22.270 --> 05:34:24.449
language models in a way that both

05:34:24.449 --> 05:34:26.789
preserves quality while minimizing copyright

05:34:26.850 --> 05:34:28.991
risk. Our

05:34:28.991 --> 05:34:30.291
work therefore leverages

05:34:31.072 --> 05:34:33.322
an emerging ecosystem of what we call safe language

05:34:33.322 --> 05:34:35.551
models. Since 2023,

05:34:35.561 --> 05:34:37.961
there has been growing development of language models

05:34:37.961 --> 05:34:39.821
trained exclusively on curated

05:34:40.181 --> 05:34:42.492
permissively licensed data. That is

05:34:42.492 --> 05:34:44.992
data not subject to restrictive copyright concerns.

05:34:45.731 --> 05:34:47.751
Conversely, risky models encompass

05:34:47.811 --> 05:34:50.082
most widely used models today. Their

05:34:50.082 --> 05:34:52.561
training data is either undisclosed or likely

05:34:52.561 --> 05:34:54.781
to include copyrighted material. In

05:34:54.781 --> 05:34:57.021
practice, these models also tend to offer

05:34:57.021 --> 05:34:59.311
higher utility and better downstream

05:34:59.311 --> 05:35:01.341
performance than safe models. And

05:35:01.341 --> 05:35:03.511
this is just to give some concrete examples.

05:35:03.672 --> 05:35:05.752
So risky models contain many popular

05:35:05.752 --> 05:35:08.072
model families that you likely use in your day to day

05:35:08.072 --> 05:35:10.321
research. Including the llama, quin,

05:35:10.321 --> 05:35:12.322
and g p t families. And in

05:35:12.322 --> 05:35:14.361
our own audits, all of them have been found to

05:35:14.361 --> 05:35:16.721
show some tangible degree of copying

05:35:16.861 --> 05:35:18.940
of protected works. But on the flip

05:35:18.940 --> 05:35:21.141
side, some safe models include the common

05:35:21.141 --> 05:35:23.301
line of models, PLAEUS, and KL3M. So these

05:35:23.301 --> 05:35:24.781
are a little bit more obscure.

05:35:25.581 --> 05:35:27.582
These are, safe models. I have all been

05:35:27.582 --> 05:35:29.651
released fairly recently, often as

05:35:29.651 --> 05:35:31.351
part of open research initiatives.

05:35:31.761 --> 05:35:33.922
And they tend to be considerably smaller than

05:35:33.922 --> 05:35:36.221
risky models. They also exhibit

05:35:36.221 --> 05:35:38.071
considerably lower copying overlap

05:35:38.231 --> 05:35:40.311
but also worse downstream performance compared to

05:35:40.311 --> 05:35:42.471
risky models. And to this end,

05:35:42.471 --> 05:35:44.731
we propose a decoding method that leverages

05:35:44.851 --> 05:35:47.031
both a safe model and a risky model.

05:35:47.191 --> 05:35:49.311
We call this anchor decoding. To put

05:35:49.311 --> 05:35:51.651
this simply, at each step, we compute

05:35:51.789 --> 05:35:53.221
a new output distribution

05:35:54.021 --> 05:35:56.260
where the distribution of the risky model is bounded to

05:35:56.260 --> 05:35:58.271
that of the safe model within a

05:35:58.271 --> 05:35:59.481
user set budget constraint.

05:36:00.520 --> 05:36:02.621
So going back to our Lord of the Rings

05:36:02.760 --> 05:36:05.041
generation example, there is an empirical

05:36:05.100 --> 05:36:07.121
trade off where neither type of language

05:36:07.180 --> 05:36:09.631
model actually produces optimal generations.

05:36:09.891 --> 05:36:12.181
So consider the input where the prompt

05:36:12.181 --> 05:36:14.341
is just the opening line of the novel, which we

05:36:14.341 --> 05:36:16.281
first pass to the risky language

05:36:16.582 --> 05:36:18.641
model. And we see that the risky

05:36:18.641 --> 05:36:20.431
language model produces an output that

05:36:20.672 --> 05:36:22.752
from a fluency perspective, it looks pretty

05:36:22.752 --> 05:36:25.101
good. But notice that the output is

05:36:25.341 --> 05:36:27.662
actually, word for word, the continuation of the next

05:36:27.662 --> 05:36:29.781
few lines of the Lord of the Rings. So

05:36:29.781 --> 05:36:31.941
from a copying perspective, this is actually

05:36:31.941 --> 05:36:32.760
pretty egregious.

05:36:34.061 --> 05:36:36.301
But on the other hand, the safe language model

05:36:36.301 --> 05:36:37.840
produces a pretty innocuous

05:36:38.541 --> 05:36:40.891
output that does not overlap with a ground truth

05:36:40.891 --> 05:36:42.930
continuation. If we look at it closely,

05:36:42.930 --> 05:36:45.011
it tends to be pretty disfluid. We

05:36:45.011 --> 05:36:47.191
can observe some degree of sentence repetition

05:36:47.521 --> 05:36:49.701
that a high utility model would not exhibit.

05:36:49.901 --> 05:36:52.061
So in all this motivates us to come up with a

05:36:52.061 --> 05:36:53.961
new decoding method that

05:36:54.101 --> 05:36:55.891
harnesses the benefits of both types of models

05:36:56.131 --> 05:36:57.911
while avoiding their respective drawbacks.

05:36:59.051 --> 05:37:01.131
Okay. So the next few slides are going to be a

05:37:01.131 --> 05:37:03.211
little bit mathematically dense. My

05:37:03.211 --> 05:37:05.291
goal is to just give the high level intuition for

05:37:05.291 --> 05:37:07.211
our method, there are additional

05:37:07.430 --> 05:37:09.510
components and full proofs, but they would be in the paper, and

05:37:09.510 --> 05:37:10.891
I'll skip them here for gravity.

05:37:11.761 --> 05:37:14.261
So one appealing feature of our decoding method

05:37:14.481 --> 05:37:16.551
is that it comes with provable guarantees. To

05:37:16.551 --> 05:37:18.971
state those guarantees, we build on a theoretical

05:37:19.029 --> 05:37:21.371
framework introduced by Viasat et

05:37:21.431 --> 05:37:23.511
al. And inspired by the legal notions

05:37:23.511 --> 05:37:25.451
of access and substantial similarity

05:37:26.082 --> 05:37:28.241
in copyright law, define a technical

05:37:28.241 --> 05:37:30.661
concept called KNAF or k near access freeness.

05:37:32.090 --> 05:37:34.251
The idea behind KNAF is simpler than

05:37:34.251 --> 05:37:36.341
the math on my slides. So

05:37:36.341 --> 05:37:38.841
basically, given a divergence function d,

05:37:38.961 --> 05:37:41.096
a non negative information budget

05:37:41.096 --> 05:37:43.231
k, and a safe reference distribution p

05:37:43.231 --> 05:37:45.361
sub s, output distribution

05:37:45.421 --> 05:37:47.541
is k nav compliant if for any

05:37:47.541 --> 05:37:49.521
possible sequence it generates,

05:37:49.681 --> 05:37:51.761
its divergence from the safe model is always

05:37:51.761 --> 05:37:54.011
bounded by k. So k is like your total budget.

05:37:54.412 --> 05:37:56.912
Importantly, this is a global sequence level

05:37:57.051 --> 05:37:59.271
constraint. That is, it applies to the entire

05:37:59.271 --> 05:38:01.351
distribution over continuations up to

05:38:01.351 --> 05:38:03.621
some length t.

05:38:03.861 --> 05:38:06.101
Rather than to individual tokens in isolation.

05:38:06.101 --> 05:38:08.181
And I'll show you that this will be kind of a problem

05:38:08.181 --> 05:38:10.321
in just a bit. But

05:38:10.321 --> 05:38:11.861
for now, we can really straightforwardly

05:38:12.461 --> 05:38:14.781
apply the idea of KNOT to our problem

05:38:14.781 --> 05:38:16.861
case, and this lends itself to a

05:38:16.861 --> 05:38:19.291
pretty natural constraint sequence level formulation,

05:38:19.741 --> 05:38:22.161
where intuitively, we want to find a new distribution,

05:38:22.381 --> 05:38:24.021
p star, that approximates

05:38:24.481 --> 05:38:26.641
p r or the risky model distribution as

05:38:26.641 --> 05:38:28.912
much as possible. Remaining bounded

05:38:28.912 --> 05:38:30.931
to the safe model distribution PS

05:38:31.301 --> 05:38:33.652
under a global user budget k. But notice

05:38:33.652 --> 05:38:35.893
that because this optimization is defined

05:38:35.893 --> 05:38:38.131
at the sequence level, solving it directly

05:38:38.131 --> 05:38:40.471
is computationally intractable for autoregressive

05:38:40.690 --> 05:38:42.781
decoding. As it then becomes a search

05:38:42.781 --> 05:38:44.002
problem over an exponentially

05:38:44.861 --> 05:38:47.100
large space. This motivates us

05:38:47.100 --> 05:38:49.180
to develop a token level local

05:38:49.180 --> 05:38:51.246
approximation that can be solved per token

05:38:51.246 --> 05:38:53.251
step instead of across the entire sequence.

05:38:53.451 --> 05:38:55.691
So notice that the token level

05:38:55.691 --> 05:38:57.731
approximation is very similar in form

05:38:57.731 --> 05:38:59.971
to the sequence level optimization in the previous

05:38:59.971 --> 05:39:02.111
slide. The difference is that we cap

05:39:02.111 --> 05:39:04.191
at a local budget k sub t instead of

05:39:04.191 --> 05:39:06.221
a large k, in the paper we actually

05:39:06.221 --> 05:39:08.381
show different ways to adaptively choose k sub

05:39:08.381 --> 05:39:10.541
t. But the bottom line is that if

05:39:10.541 --> 05:39:12.621
you choose k sub t in a way such that they

05:39:12.621 --> 05:39:13.831
sum up to large k

05:39:15.190 --> 05:39:17.610
then this token level local approximation satisfies

05:39:17.671 --> 05:39:20.101
the global sequence level ground, and therefore

05:39:20.101 --> 05:39:22.422
is KNAV compliant. And in

05:39:22.563 --> 05:39:24.902
principle, because the math works itself out really cleanly,

05:39:25.451 --> 05:39:27.391
we basically can solve this optimization

05:39:27.531 --> 05:39:29.590
at each step and get a pair of weights

05:39:29.590 --> 05:39:31.661
for both the safe and risky model. Which

05:39:31.661 --> 05:39:33.921
we can then use to obtain a fused distribution

05:39:34.141 --> 05:39:36.181
from the two models that we ultimately sample

05:39:36.181 --> 05:39:38.311
from. Okay. So

05:39:38.311 --> 05:39:40.501
now we're done with the math part, which is good.

05:39:40.881 --> 05:39:43.381
But, qualitatively speaking, how good is anchor decoding?

05:39:43.691 --> 05:39:45.771
So going back to our Lord of the Rings

05:39:45.771 --> 05:39:48.041
example, we can see that the resulting output

05:39:48.041 --> 05:39:50.201
is pretty good, at least in in my biased

05:39:50.201 --> 05:39:52.411
opinion. It seems pretty original,

05:39:52.471 --> 05:39:54.471
I. E. Not derivative of any works

05:39:54.791 --> 05:39:57.141
protected under copyright. And the quality is

05:39:57.141 --> 05:39:59.481
also much better relative to the safe model's output.

05:40:00.770 --> 05:40:03.010
But before we get into empirical results, during

05:40:03.010 --> 05:40:05.221
the process, we came across some practical

05:40:05.279 --> 05:40:07.680
challenges in our setting. And just to begin,

05:40:07.680 --> 05:40:10.010
like many two model fusion methods,

05:40:10.171 --> 05:40:12.430
such as contrastive decoding among others,

05:40:12.771 --> 05:40:14.851
anchor decoding assumes that the model pairs

05:40:14.851 --> 05:40:16.971
are tokenizer compatible. A

05:40:16.971 --> 05:40:18.981
sense that they must share a common vocabulary

05:40:18.981 --> 05:40:21.351
space. And this is actually really tricky

05:40:21.351 --> 05:40:23.361
for our use case as the safe

05:40:23.361 --> 05:40:25.521
models that are available tend to have bespoke

05:40:25.521 --> 05:40:27.662
tokenizers are not directly

05:40:27.721 --> 05:40:29.951
compatible with risky model families. So for

05:40:29.951 --> 05:40:32.131
this problem, we have to propose two solutions.

05:40:33.061 --> 05:40:35.381
And the first workaround is that for a decoding paper,

05:40:35.381 --> 05:40:37.081
we pre train our own language model.

05:40:37.761 --> 05:40:39.841
We pretrained our own safe model tiny

05:40:39.841 --> 05:40:41.241
comma 1.8 b

05:40:41.881 --> 05:40:44.221
that is tokenizer compatible with a LAMA three family.

05:40:44.651 --> 05:40:46.430
Full training details are in the paper

05:40:46.661 --> 05:40:48.741
but we basically did standard pretraining on

05:40:48.741 --> 05:40:50.851
the common pile, which is the largest

05:40:51.151 --> 05:40:52.481
permissible license dataset to date.

05:40:53.201 --> 05:40:55.441
And just to show some really quick results,

05:40:55.441 --> 05:40:57.931
we evaluated tiny, comma, 1.8

05:40:57.931 --> 05:41:00.011
b against a range of safe models as well as a

05:41:00.011 --> 05:41:02.231
few risky models. It performs

05:41:02.231 --> 05:41:04.311
pretty well in a sense that it beats out several

05:41:04.311 --> 05:41:06.541
slightly larger safe models, and

05:41:06.541 --> 05:41:08.651
catches up to the larger seven b comma models.

05:41:08.971 --> 05:41:11.371
However, while tiny comma 1.8

05:41:11.371 --> 05:41:13.440
b enables us to do anchor decoding

05:41:13.440 --> 05:41:15.681
with a LAMA three models, That's only

05:41:15.681 --> 05:41:17.770
one model family. The more ambitious question

05:41:17.770 --> 05:41:19.471
is, how can we enable compatibility

05:41:19.930 --> 05:41:21.761
with any safe risky model pair?

05:41:22.801 --> 05:41:25.281
So the second solution is to convert our decoding

05:41:25.281 --> 05:41:27.461
algorithm from token level to the byte

05:41:27.461 --> 05:41:29.801
level. And this kind of exploits the observation

05:41:29.941 --> 05:41:31.971
that many modern tokenizers are

05:41:31.971 --> 05:41:34.131
byte pair encoding based, this allows

05:41:34.131 --> 05:41:36.551
us to use ByteSampler, a framework introduced

05:41:36.610 --> 05:41:38.761
by my second author John in an earlier

05:41:38.761 --> 05:41:41.051
paper that converts any BPA tokenizer

05:41:41.351 --> 05:41:43.501
based language model into a byte level model

05:41:43.661 --> 05:41:45.981
while preserving its exact generated distribution

05:41:45.981 --> 05:41:47.983
at the text level. And I'm not going to

05:41:47.983 --> 05:41:50.141
go into too many details here, but essentially

05:41:50.141 --> 05:41:52.551
you can think of byte sampler as a black

05:41:52.551 --> 05:41:54.631
box function takes in any language

05:41:54.690 --> 05:41:56.631
model and returns an invariant byte language

05:41:56.850 --> 05:41:58.492
model. And instead of working

05:41:59.031 --> 05:42:01.271
in a vocabulary space, we can now do all operations

05:42:01.271 --> 05:42:03.340
in a byte space. And since that's basically

05:42:03.340 --> 05:42:05.341
the only change, and we show this in

05:42:05.341 --> 05:42:07.760
more detail in our paper, The k knot guarantee

05:42:07.760 --> 05:42:08.741
still holds theoretically.

05:42:10.411 --> 05:42:12.351
So just looking at empirical results,

05:42:12.931 --> 05:42:14.711
we report an aggregate of various

05:42:15.011 --> 05:42:17.152
literal copying metrics on popular

05:42:17.371 --> 05:42:19.072
copyright protected novels from CopyBench.

05:42:19.521 --> 05:42:21.551
Which is a copyright measurement

05:42:21.781 --> 05:42:23.791
dataset. And we look at

05:42:23.791 --> 05:42:25.893
copyright copying risk against two notions

05:42:25.951 --> 05:42:28.061
of utility. Long form factuality

05:42:28.199 --> 05:42:30.141
using fax score as well as fluency.

05:42:30.761 --> 05:42:32.851
We sweep various budgets of k, which

05:42:32.851 --> 05:42:35.001
impact practice is a user controllable

05:42:35.141 --> 05:42:36.921
knob for the allowable risk budget.

05:42:37.431 --> 05:42:39.511
We can see that in our token level

05:42:39.511 --> 05:42:41.600
setting, anchor decoding actually works

05:42:41.600 --> 05:42:43.860
quite well across both utility cases.

05:42:44.061 --> 05:42:46.141
It's just like the purple line, and we can see that

05:42:46.141 --> 05:42:48.332
