what makes benchmarks really effective,

06:54:07.630 --> 06:54:09.900
as measuring sticks in particular, the

06:54:09.900 --> 06:54:11.980
one that we really often focus on is

06:54:11.980 --> 06:54:14.240
what the quality of the data and the tasks

06:54:14.550 --> 06:54:16.580
are really like. And of course, quality is something

06:54:16.580 --> 06:54:19.080
that you have to go measure in numerous ways.

06:54:19.570 --> 06:54:21.990
It's also something that's really evolved pretty significantly

06:54:22.210 --> 06:54:24.230
in the past. So the one kind of

06:54:24.230 --> 06:54:26.470
benchmark I love and that I'll highlight is GPU

06:54:26.470 --> 06:54:28.411
QA. And it's sort of

06:54:28.550 --> 06:54:30.689
one of these benchmarks that tracks the evolution

06:54:31.171 --> 06:54:33.180
of what you see from the past. Which

06:54:33.180 --> 06:54:35.320
were often kind of much more

06:54:35.320 --> 06:54:37.350
simpler types of benchmarks. To something

06:54:37.350 --> 06:54:39.650
that's now very sophisticated to build in order

06:54:39.890 --> 06:54:41.740
to maintain the highest possible quality.

06:54:41.980 --> 06:54:44.140
So I think if you go back in time and look at early

06:54:44.140 --> 06:54:46.510
QA efforts, a lot of those benchmarks really

06:54:46.570 --> 06:54:48.670
came down to let's scrape some questions off

06:54:48.670 --> 06:54:50.830
the Internet and get as much coverage as we possibly

06:54:50.830 --> 06:54:53.070
can. Maybe there wasn't such a focus at

06:54:53.070 --> 06:54:55.150
the time on making sure those questions weren't

06:54:55.150 --> 06:54:57.298
correct. Unambiguous, etcetera.

06:54:57.840 --> 06:55:00.310
And as a result of that, these things are very useful

06:55:00.310 --> 06:55:02.380
and shape the field but they often had

06:55:02.380 --> 06:55:04.511
some problems. And what I like about

06:55:04.511 --> 06:55:06.830
things like GPQA is that the offers have really

06:55:06.830 --> 06:55:09.060
fought this fruit quite a lot. And they created

06:55:09.060 --> 06:55:11.120
this really complex system

06:55:11.420 --> 06:55:13.530
of incentives to try to make sure that the problems they

06:55:13.530 --> 06:55:15.609
built were actually perfectly correct,

06:55:15.609 --> 06:55:17.640
unambiguous, and so forth. So

06:55:17.640 --> 06:55:19.720
the way they actually do this is basically they hire

06:55:19.720 --> 06:55:21.660
creators or offers for these problems

06:55:21.970 --> 06:55:24.190
along with expert reviewers Now

06:55:24.190 --> 06:55:26.020
unfortunately, the typical incentive

06:55:26.261 --> 06:55:28.560
is if you pay these folks by the hour, they'll try

06:55:28.560 --> 06:55:30.640
to produce as much stuff as possible and that

06:55:30.640 --> 06:55:32.700
will be it. Right? The offers will create lots of

06:55:32.700 --> 06:55:34.740
questions. The reviewers will review

06:55:34.740 --> 06:55:37.100
and pass lots of these questions as a result.

06:55:37.421 --> 06:55:39.581
So what these folks have done is really build the system

06:55:39.581 --> 06:55:41.850
of incentives. So for example, they'll

06:55:41.850 --> 06:55:44.118
do things like if the offer writes

06:55:44.118 --> 06:55:46.070
a question, they get paid a certain amount.

06:55:46.400 --> 06:55:48.560
But if that question is passed by

06:55:48.560 --> 06:55:50.260
reviewers, they get a bonus, for example.

06:55:50.790 --> 06:55:52.730
If the reviewers all agree independently,

06:55:53.341 --> 06:55:55.421
that there is something valuable in a question, they also

06:55:55.421 --> 06:55:57.560
get a bonus. If they all agree there's something bad

06:55:57.560 --> 06:55:59.800
or flawed about a question, they end up getting a bonus

06:55:59.800 --> 06:56:01.822
as well. So it's this really intricate system

06:56:01.880 --> 06:56:04.171
of economic incentives It really

06:56:04.171 --> 06:56:06.350
is derived from things like mechanism design,

06:56:06.540 --> 06:56:08.620
but it's become incredibly powerful in

06:56:08.620 --> 06:56:10.740
data creation. You can't really get by

06:56:10.740 --> 06:56:12.890
without having this kind of system. So this is something

06:56:12.890 --> 06:56:15.050
that I've really appreciated, and it's inspired a lot of

06:56:15.050 --> 06:56:16.870
the ways that we create our benchmarks too.

06:56:17.671 --> 06:56:19.751
Kind of moving on toward diversity. So I think,

06:56:19.751 --> 06:56:21.822
of course, this is one of the most important aspects

06:56:21.822 --> 06:56:24.220
of building a good benchmark. So we'll highlight

06:56:24.220 --> 06:56:26.260
the classic here like MMLU, where

06:56:26.260 --> 06:56:27.960
they had a huge number of categories.

06:56:28.690 --> 06:56:30.850
Different questions in each category based on kind of what

06:56:30.850 --> 06:56:32.982
typical users see. We often

06:56:32.982 --> 06:56:35.060
do things like define a taxonomy and

06:56:35.060 --> 06:56:35.970
sort of very

06:56:39.770 --> 06:56:41.950
Difficulty, of course, is the most interesting and most

06:56:42.350 --> 06:56:44.450
challenging aspect of building benchmarks today.

06:56:44.670 --> 06:56:46.750
So we want to create questions that are hard enough

06:56:46.750 --> 06:56:49.150
for models that there's enough room for models

06:56:49.150 --> 06:56:51.341
to climb so they're not saturated. On

06:56:51.341 --> 06:56:53.501
the other hand, these questions have to be valid.

06:56:53.501 --> 06:56:55.689
Right? So you can't cheat by creating

06:56:55.689 --> 06:56:58.109
impossible questions in order to meet this difficulty

06:56:58.410 --> 06:57:00.440
bar. So RKGI has been really great

06:57:00.440 --> 06:57:02.480
at this. That's one of the benchmarks that we're

06:57:02.480 --> 06:57:04.950
often inspired by. They often start with

06:57:05.010 --> 06:57:07.250
versions that have something like one or 2%

06:57:07.250 --> 06:57:09.580
accuracy. So are really challenging for models,

06:57:09.580 --> 06:57:11.740
and then only gradually do you see solutions that

06:57:11.740 --> 06:57:13.902
start really climbing there. So they

06:57:13.902 --> 06:57:15.761
are really amazing at building unsaturated

06:57:15.982 --> 06:57:18.080
benchmarks. And finally, of course,

06:57:18.080 --> 06:57:20.020
making sure that you have a robust methodology

06:57:20.430 --> 06:57:22.380
for evaluation is equally important

06:57:22.591 --> 06:57:24.671
is something that we spend a lot of time on, and I'll

06:57:24.671 --> 06:57:26.831
give a shout out to Tao Bench for inspiring a lot

06:57:26.831 --> 06:57:28.710
of what we end up doing at Snorkel as well.

06:57:29.270 --> 06:57:31.430
So all of these facets are really important to take

06:57:31.430 --> 06:57:33.880
into account They'll all produce benchmarks

06:57:33.940 --> 06:57:36.200
that are really usable and that tell you good information.

06:57:36.940 --> 06:57:39.261
You have to kind of go beyond this if you're interested

06:57:39.261 --> 06:57:41.280
in building a benchmark that really potentially

06:57:41.710 --> 06:57:43.870
changes or shapes the field. So this

06:57:43.870 --> 06:57:46.140
is more getting into the art It's

06:57:46.140 --> 06:57:48.640
really about sort of guessing what will come next.

06:57:48.880 --> 06:57:50.900
The frontier is gonna look like in the future,

06:57:51.140 --> 06:57:53.161
and building something around that. So

06:57:53.161 --> 06:57:55.160
this is something much more difficult in a way

06:57:55.480 --> 06:57:57.720
Our thinking is that there's a couple of things

06:57:57.720 --> 06:57:59.980
in common for all the real game changing

06:57:59.980 --> 06:58:01.991
benchmarks. If you have these,

06:58:01.991 --> 06:58:03.640
there's a good shot you have at doing it.

06:58:04.120 --> 06:58:06.261
So for example, most of these

06:58:06.261 --> 06:58:08.360
benchmarks, they have a real thesis

06:58:08.420 --> 06:58:10.200
about where the field is gonna go.

06:58:10.440 --> 06:58:12.650
And they make a bet on it. And so

06:58:12.650 --> 06:58:14.810
for one example, we love terminal bench.

06:58:14.810 --> 06:58:17.130
And here's a quote from Mike Merrill, who's won the

06:58:17.130 --> 06:58:19.210
creators of TerminalBench. Right? He basically

06:58:19.210 --> 06:58:21.380
says, our big bet is that in

06:58:21.380 --> 06:58:23.560
the future, something like 99%

06:58:23.560 --> 06:58:25.980
or 95% of LLM computer interaction

06:58:26.530 --> 06:58:28.770
is through the terminal. And that's something that we've

06:58:28.770 --> 06:58:31.010
really gotten accustomed to lately, but it wasn't

06:58:31.010 --> 06:58:33.090
really an obvious thing given the huge

06:58:33.090 --> 06:58:34.570
space of options you could have.

06:58:35.150 --> 06:58:37.330
But making this bet and really intuiting

06:58:37.390 --> 06:58:39.429
what was going to happen the future has made things like

06:58:39.429 --> 06:58:41.050
terminal bench incredibly impactful.

06:58:41.860 --> 06:58:43.940
Similarly, if you have a good road map for

06:58:43.940 --> 06:58:46.390
the field, think that keeps a lot of these benchmarks

06:58:46.390 --> 06:58:48.400
quite fresh and useful. So of

06:58:48.400 --> 06:58:50.480
course, sweep bench has been incredibly important as

06:58:50.480 --> 06:58:52.590
well. But one thing we love about it is that it

06:58:52.590 --> 06:58:54.810
wasn't sort of a static single benchmark.

06:58:54.810 --> 06:58:56.970
That was there. It actually was something

06:58:56.970 --> 06:58:59.150
that grew and evolved with a bunch of different

06:58:59.370 --> 06:59:01.460
versions that were tackling different needs.

06:59:01.460 --> 06:59:03.620
So the original sweet bench, sweet bench light

06:59:03.620 --> 06:59:05.630
verified these newer multilingual

06:59:05.630 --> 06:59:07.779
and multimodal versions. All of these things

06:59:07.779 --> 06:59:09.780
have sort of gotten added value over

06:59:09.780 --> 06:59:11.960
time. Have kept it useful for many,

06:59:11.960 --> 06:59:13.990
many years. Similarly, we

06:59:13.990 --> 06:59:16.070
think there's an aspect of how well

06:59:16.070 --> 06:59:18.122
researchers can interact with this kind

06:59:18.122 --> 06:59:19.940
of work that makes for a really impactful

06:59:20.402 --> 06:59:22.610
benchmark. Just a couple

06:59:22.610 --> 06:59:24.690
of things that I'll highlight here. So for example, Helm and its common

06:59:24.690 --> 06:59:26.741
interface was super useful.

06:59:26.960 --> 06:59:29.200
And similarly, Harbor, which is a framework for

06:59:29.200 --> 06:59:31.250
agents that was built alongside terminal

06:59:31.250 --> 06:59:33.618
bench, has really kind of made things easy

06:59:33.700 --> 06:59:35.560
a lot of people to go do experiments.

06:59:35.930 --> 06:59:38.190
So having make sure that this kind of frictionless

06:59:38.331 --> 06:59:40.411
environment exists for researchers to use your

06:59:40.411 --> 06:59:42.490
benchmarks we think that's something that really

06:59:42.490 --> 06:59:43.990
produces a lot of value.

06:59:44.790 --> 06:59:46.950
So this is kind of our our general hot takes on

06:59:46.950 --> 06:59:49.190
what makes for a good benchmark. And really what

06:59:49.190 --> 06:59:51.460
drives these things forward. We've also

06:59:51.460 --> 06:59:53.850
recently been thinking quite a lot about things like

06:59:54.491 --> 06:59:56.350
four agentic benchmarks in particular.

06:59:56.930 --> 06:59:59.070
What should these things have? What

06:59:59.070 --> 07:00:00.449
will really make them useful?

07:00:01.120 --> 07:00:03.160
How do we create the taxonomy that

07:00:03.160 --> 07:00:05.320
kind of specifies what matters and perhaps

07:00:05.320 --> 07:00:07.500
what doesn't for building a good agentic

07:00:07.500 --> 07:00:09.580
benchmark. And we've come up with this kind

07:00:09.580 --> 07:00:11.591
of free access definition There's

07:00:11.591 --> 07:00:13.650
lots of ways to kind of slice it. But

07:00:13.650 --> 07:00:13.730
we

07:00:21.358 --> 07:00:23.698
think this is one of the simplest ways that can characterize

07:00:23.998 --> 07:00:26.318
a good agentic benchmark. We

07:00:26.318 --> 07:00:28.258
start from something like environment complexity.

07:00:28.398 --> 07:00:30.158
Right? So how rich and how dynamic

07:00:30.639 --> 07:00:33.028
is the environment that our agents are gonna operate in?

07:00:33.588 --> 07:00:35.828
How autonomous are gonna be these agents,

07:00:35.828 --> 07:00:37.998
so our autonomy horizon, how independent do

07:00:37.998 --> 07:00:40.078
we want them to be? And finally, we look at

07:00:40.078 --> 07:00:42.328
their outputs, how rich, how complex are the outputs

07:00:42.328 --> 07:00:44.418
from these agents. So just kind

07:00:44.418 --> 07:00:46.758
of walking through these, and I'll give an example

07:00:46.828 --> 07:00:49.168
a new benchmark we're building that I'm very excited

07:00:49.228 --> 07:00:51.658
by. So environment complexity is, of

07:00:51.658 --> 07:00:53.658
course, incredibly important. Because

07:00:54.139 --> 07:00:56.158
ultimately, there's a big gap between the complexity

07:00:56.299 --> 07:00:58.477
of the real world actual deployments

07:00:58.477 --> 07:01:00.608
of agents, and what we build in

07:01:00.608 --> 07:01:02.709
any kind of benchmark, any kind of system.

07:01:03.018 --> 07:01:05.338
So here, for example, imagine that we're using

07:01:05.338 --> 07:01:07.608
an agent to do something like you know,

07:01:07.608 --> 07:01:09.468
introduce new users to a system.

07:01:09.728 --> 07:01:11.868
Onboard them, Right? There's all kinds

07:01:11.868 --> 07:01:13.998
of crazy situations that will happen there

07:01:14.158 --> 07:01:16.184
something that can take a long time have

07:01:16.184 --> 07:01:18.348
many many subtasks, might be all kinds

07:01:18.348 --> 07:01:20.428
of changes to the company, the schema, etcetera,

07:01:20.428 --> 07:01:22.048
that take place during this process.

07:01:22.469 --> 07:01:24.709
These are all complexities that are not sort of obvious

07:01:24.709 --> 07:01:26.798
for us to build but are incredibly important if you

07:01:26.798 --> 07:01:28.578
wanna have something truly realistic.

07:01:28.998 --> 07:01:31.238
So some angles that we really care about for these,

07:01:31.238 --> 07:01:33.398
right, how domain specific is

07:01:33.398 --> 07:01:35.458
the benchmark gonna be? And

07:01:35.598 --> 07:01:37.758
ultimately, how much real knowledge is not

07:01:37.758 --> 07:01:39.788
stored any particular place,

07:01:39.788 --> 07:01:41.889
but rather in the minds of people, are

07:01:41.889 --> 07:01:43.538
things that we often end up caring about.

07:01:44.098 --> 07:01:46.178
Similarly, how complex is

07:01:46.178 --> 07:01:48.227
the context? Multimodal is this

07:01:48.227 --> 07:01:50.388
gonna be? Our favorite which is tool

07:01:50.388 --> 07:01:52.768
complexity, A lot of our different agentic

07:01:52.768 --> 07:01:54.848
systems ultimately struggle the most with tool

07:01:54.848 --> 07:01:56.987
usage. It's still a big challenge for

07:01:56.987 --> 07:01:59.147
agents who will often hallucinate tools or

07:01:59.147 --> 07:02:01.469
fail to use them reliably. And then finally,

07:02:01.469 --> 07:02:03.868
how we interact with other agents and also human

07:02:03.868 --> 07:02:05.818
users both in terms of

07:02:05.958 --> 07:02:08.158
coordination complexity, and how complex

07:02:08.158 --> 07:02:10.307
that communication will be. So

07:02:10.307 --> 07:02:12.548
turning to autonomy, this is basically just a

07:02:12.548 --> 07:02:14.719
question of how long do we expect this

07:02:14.719 --> 07:02:16.738
agent to be able to sort of adequately

07:02:16.799 --> 07:02:18.898
continue before it really breaks down.

07:02:18.988 --> 07:02:21.032
Is it going to be something that's going to

07:02:21.032 --> 07:02:22.848
take tens or hundreds or thousands of steps?

07:02:23.358 --> 07:02:25.518
How complex is that world going to be?

07:02:25.518 --> 07:02:27.808
How much do our agents need access to

07:02:27.808 --> 07:02:30.138
a world model? To efficiently explore?

07:02:30.138 --> 07:02:32.557
And finally, these goals that they're going to be tackling

07:02:32.868 --> 07:02:34.948
how non stationary? How diverse are they gonna

07:02:34.948 --> 07:02:37.128
be? How much will they change over time as well?

07:02:37.608 --> 07:02:39.688
So this is something that really characterizes a

07:02:39.688 --> 07:02:41.778
lot of kind of what we expect our agents to

07:02:41.778 --> 07:02:44.148
be able to do. And then finally,

07:02:44.148 --> 07:02:45.858
the output complexity is sort of the most

07:02:46.178 --> 07:02:47.998
obvious one here, Right? How sophisticated

07:02:48.218 --> 07:02:50.318
is the output, the deliverable, gonna be?

07:02:50.548 --> 07:02:52.678
Our agents are gonna produce? Increasingly,

07:02:52.818 --> 07:02:55.057
our agents are not expected to just sort of do

07:02:55.057 --> 07:02:57.168
one particular thing. They have much

07:02:57.168 --> 07:02:59.246
more multifaceted outputs that they have to

07:02:59.246 --> 07:03:01.416
be able to produce. We have to build

07:03:01.416 --> 07:03:03.238
very nuanced rubrics and rewards

07:03:03.558 --> 07:03:05.719
systems for them. Otherwise, we'll immediately struggle

07:03:05.719 --> 07:03:08.049
to do anything. Of course, our outputs also

07:03:08.108 --> 07:03:10.139
have to be very trustworthy. So this is

07:03:10.139 --> 07:03:12.219
kind of all I wanted to really chat about in

07:03:12.219 --> 07:03:14.278
terms of sort of how we see the space

07:03:14.278 --> 07:03:16.378
of benchmarks, especially for agents,

07:03:17.038 --> 07:03:19.278
also want to use one particular example that we're

07:03:19.278 --> 07:03:21.407
working on both in my academic group,

07:03:21.407 --> 07:03:23.506
but also with snorkel's open benchmark

07:03:23.567 --> 07:03:25.508
program. And it's called Slopcode

07:03:25.647 --> 07:03:27.678
bench. We picked this name kind

07:03:27.678 --> 07:03:29.918
of to be as provocative as possible after

07:03:29.918 --> 07:03:31.938
exploring a big space of them. But

07:03:31.938 --> 07:03:34.018
the basic motivation is really the fact

07:03:34.018 --> 07:03:36.068
that AI is producing vast

07:03:36.068 --> 07:03:38.408
amounts of slot really across

07:03:38.468 --> 07:03:40.628
every kind of data modality that you can think

07:03:40.628 --> 07:03:42.369
of. And this is a

07:03:42.668 --> 07:03:44.727
problem everywhere. But maybe, you know, having

07:03:44.727 --> 07:03:46.807
to see too many annoying fake AI

07:03:46.807 --> 07:03:48.950
videos on Twitter is not as

07:03:49.008 --> 07:03:51.088
damaging as what we get as a result of

07:03:51.088 --> 07:03:53.368
code slop. So we've seen actually tons

07:03:53.368 --> 07:03:55.518
of issues that have come up now that anybody

07:03:55.518 --> 07:03:57.678
can run their code agent and produce whatever

07:03:57.678 --> 07:04:00.119
output they like. There are, for example, organizations

07:04:00.738 --> 07:04:02.918
that no longer work with external contributors.

07:04:03.228 --> 07:04:05.098
They no longer take PRs.

07:04:05.258 --> 07:04:07.498
Because of the fact they're being inundated with tons

07:04:07.498 --> 07:04:09.608
and tons of code slop. And it

07:04:09.608 --> 07:04:11.928
takes a long time to review and to make sure anything

07:04:11.928 --> 07:04:14.049
there is reasonable. This is kind of

07:04:14.049 --> 07:04:16.100
one of the challenges that have come with

07:04:16.100 --> 07:04:17.588
these amazing dramatic advancements

07:04:18.299 --> 07:04:20.798
in code agents, especially in the last six months.

07:04:21.428 --> 07:04:23.668
So the the first question that we really started thinking

07:04:23.668 --> 07:04:25.688
about when building this benchmark is

07:04:25.828 --> 07:04:28.198
how do we actually go measure SLOP?

07:04:28.278 --> 07:04:30.358
In fact, what does swap really even

07:04:30.358 --> 07:04:31.888
mean in any sense here?

07:04:32.368 --> 07:04:34.448
And one thing that we really kind of came up with

07:04:34.448 --> 07:04:36.928
as a central property is

07:04:36.987 --> 07:04:39.088
something like SLOP should be something measured

07:04:39.468 --> 07:04:40.688
over iterative process

07:04:41.568 --> 07:04:43.598
So you have two examples that you see here.

07:04:43.918 --> 07:04:46.238
Of just some simple functions that a code agent

07:04:46.238 --> 07:04:48.477
wrote. The first one's in response to

07:04:48.477 --> 07:04:50.258
being asked to do some kind of search.

07:04:50.498 --> 07:04:52.619
It's an okay function. As

07:04:52.619 --> 07:04:55.039
a single sort of time point, as a single request.

07:04:55.518 --> 07:04:57.598
It's maybe okay for our purposes. Maybe this

07:04:57.598 --> 07:04:59.928
is not really where swap is such a big deal.

07:05:00.578 --> 07:05:02.818
The real problem starts happening when we ask

07:05:02.818 --> 07:05:04.898
the model to do something different starting

07:05:04.898 --> 07:05:07.388
from that point. When we sort of add a

07:05:07.448 --> 07:05:09.748
second, a third, a fourth point, when we have

07:05:09.748 --> 07:05:11.768
this more long running iterative process,

07:05:12.088 --> 07:05:14.328
then we really see slops starting to take

07:05:14.328 --> 07:05:16.388
hold. So for example, if you make some

07:05:16.388 --> 07:05:18.248
modification to what you did initially,

07:05:18.608 --> 07:05:21.008
the agent doesn't really know how to make the simplest

07:05:21.008 --> 07:05:22.868
and most effective possible modification

07:05:23.078 --> 07:05:25.307
to the code. It'll start adding a bunch

07:05:25.307 --> 07:05:27.628
of stuff. It doesn't think through very carefully

07:05:27.628 --> 07:05:29.708
about how to refactor code. It just says, let me

07:05:29.708 --> 07:05:32.038
add some more cases and I'll handle whatever

07:05:32.038 --> 07:05:34.077
I'm being asked to handle. So kind

07:05:34.077 --> 07:05:36.157
of taking this example and trying

07:05:36.157 --> 07:05:38.528
to build more of a knock actual framework

07:05:38.827 --> 07:05:40.838
around it, we came up with something that looks a little bit like

07:05:40.838 --> 07:05:42.977
this. We have some initial

07:05:42.977 --> 07:05:45.358
spec that you see on the left over there Let's suppose

07:05:45.358 --> 07:05:47.438
that it's an example like doing some basic

07:05:47.438 --> 07:05:49.557
code search. And our agent does a reasonable

07:05:49.557 --> 07:05:51.758
job It produces a solution with

07:05:51.758 --> 07:05:54.258
two files over there, and maybe the quality of the solution

07:05:54.408 --> 07:05:56.538
is reasonable. Then we ask

07:05:56.538 --> 07:05:58.678
the agent to do some modification which is

07:05:58.678 --> 07:06:00.758
sort of tracking a very realistic standard

07:06:00.758 --> 07:06:02.938
process that we typically have in software development.

07:06:03.538 --> 07:06:05.858
Adding some new features. Right? So for example, maybe

07:06:05.858 --> 07:06:07.718
we handle some new programming languages

07:06:08.308 --> 07:06:10.389
this case, the agent says, okay, let me

07:06:10.389 --> 07:06:12.288
add some more to take care of these new

07:06:15.487 --> 07:06:15.987
So

07:06:17.498 --> 07:06:19.578
This is probably not necessary. We could probably

07:06:19.578 --> 07:06:21.838
refactor and do it with a much smaller delta

07:06:22.128 --> 07:06:24.147
but instead we're just adding more lines.

07:06:24.418 --> 07:06:26.678
And we keep doing this with more and more updates,

07:06:26.738 --> 07:06:28.918
each time creating the sort of massive

07:06:29.488 --> 07:06:31.618
construct that becomes incredibly difficult

07:06:31.618 --> 07:06:33.698
to maintain, to review, to extend,

07:06:33.698 --> 07:06:35.897
and so forth. This is the basic

07:06:35.897 --> 07:06:37.938
idea. We went forth and kind of built

07:06:37.938 --> 07:06:40.278
an actual benchmark around this idea.

07:06:40.618 --> 07:06:42.938
All of our problems are basically check pointed,

07:06:42.938 --> 07:06:44.317
so they have multiple iterations.

07:06:45.028 --> 07:06:47.108
The basic idea is just that we give a natural

07:06:47.108 --> 07:06:49.141
language specification to our agent. We

07:06:49.141 --> 07:06:51.168
have a set of tests that we can use to measure

07:06:51.168 --> 07:06:53.227
performance. And then each agent is

07:06:53.227 --> 07:06:55.008
just basically given the natural

07:06:55.388 --> 07:06:56.918
language for the next iteration

07:06:57.958 --> 07:07:00.178
along with the workspace that it's performed so far.

07:07:00.338 --> 07:07:02.498
So you just basically have everything the agent's done

07:07:02.498 --> 07:07:04.578
so far plus the new thing it's being asked to

07:07:04.578 --> 07:07:06.718
do and we keep track of what's going on

07:07:06.718 --> 07:07:08.878
there. Alright. So we're at

07:07:08.878 --> 07:07:11.308
DataFM. And so far, I haven't really talked about data

07:07:11.308 --> 07:07:13.469
too much. So where is our data

07:07:13.469 --> 07:07:15.528
coming from? Turns out that building these

07:07:15.528 --> 07:07:17.848
kinds of checkpointed problems is not super

07:07:17.848 --> 07:07:19.948
trivial. Haven't built a huge number

07:07:19.948 --> 07:07:22.108
because each one has multiple components. So we're

07:07:22.108 --> 07:07:24.358
at 40 and growing so far. In

07:07:24.358 --> 07:07:26.688
fact, if anybody has contributions they'd like to make,

07:07:27.008 --> 07:07:29.248
would be wonderful. Just a shameless plug there.

07:07:29.408 --> 07:07:31.889
Most of these problems while they have some synthetic

07:07:31.948 --> 07:07:33.728
components, a lot of them are really manually

07:07:34.313 --> 07:07:36.488
built. They have to be sort of carefully designed

07:07:36.488 --> 07:07:38.808
to make sure they make sense. Then we verify

07:07:38.808 --> 07:07:40.838
them all manually as well. So we have

07:07:40.838 --> 07:07:43.237
a bunch of examples over here, including some truly strange

07:07:43.237 --> 07:07:45.418
ones. We've done a bunch of tasks that are like inspired

07:07:45.418 --> 07:07:47.598
from video games that have incredibly high complexity.

07:07:47.908 --> 07:07:49.588
And actually these agents are quite bad on.

07:07:50.448 --> 07:07:52.078
How do we actually measure swap

07:07:52.658 --> 07:07:54.838
the other sort of question that's interesting here is

07:07:55.078 --> 07:07:57.098
there's some interplay, right, between slop

07:07:57.498 --> 07:07:59.738
and performance. You could have sloppy

07:07:59.738 --> 07:08:01.808
code that still works That still solves

07:08:01.808 --> 07:08:03.968
the problem. You could have code that

07:08:03.968 --> 07:08:06.048
has no slop and is very nice but does not

07:08:06.048 --> 07:08:08.358
solve the problem. One thing that we really

07:08:08.358 --> 07:08:10.299
wanted to know was what is this interaction

07:08:10.678 --> 07:08:13.008
between these two metrics what should we expect

07:08:13.008 --> 07:08:15.048
from it? So we basically built

07:08:15.048 --> 07:08:17.128
metrics for SLOP. I won't give

07:08:17.128 --> 07:08:19.219
a precise definition here. But a lot of what

07:08:19.219 --> 07:08:21.299
we've built is basically inspired from old

07:08:21.299 --> 07:08:23.318
old constructs from software development.

07:08:23.938 --> 07:08:26.258
Things like cyclomatic complexity. But basically,

07:08:26.258 --> 07:08:28.278
we've defined sort of two notions of swap.

07:08:28.528 --> 07:08:30.688
One is a bit more harmful. The one on the left here

07:08:30.688 --> 07:08:32.950
is structural erosion. This basically

07:08:32.950 --> 07:08:35.028
says that you're not really doing the right

07:08:35.028 --> 07:08:37.088
thing. So the slop has really produced some

07:08:37.088 --> 07:08:39.108
kind of fundamentally more bad outcomes

07:08:39.248 --> 07:08:41.209
here. You have some very complex code

07:08:41.518 --> 07:08:43.458
that isn't really doing the things you'd expect.

07:08:43.618 --> 07:08:45.778
And then we have sort of more surface level slop

07:08:45.778 --> 07:08:47.958
that's a little bit more benign on the right.

07:08:48.278 --> 07:08:50.378
Annoying, for example, if you have totally redundant

07:08:50.438 --> 07:08:52.778
functions. Like, could potentially be dangerous.

07:08:52.938 --> 07:08:55.208
But it's not quite as bad as what you see on the left.

